window.IPT_INDEX=[{"u":"news/2026-10-14-two-palestinians-hit-in-the-head-by-rubber-coated.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"00:10","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Two Palestinians hit in the head by rubber-coated bullets at village gate, sources say","g":"checkpoint","x":"They were trying to cross the iron gate that has long sealed the road between Ras Karkar and Kharbatha Bani Harith, local sources told WAFA.","s":"WAFA (English)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Ras Karkar, west of Ramallah","b":"Israeli forces fired rubber-coated metal bullets at two Palestinians late on Tuesday as they tried to cross the iron gate separating the lands of Ras Karkar, west of Ramallah, from the neighbouring village of Kharbatha Bani Harith, hitting both in the head, local sources told WAFA. They were taken to Ramallah for treatment. The sources said Israeli forces have kept the road between the two villages closed with the gate for a long time, barring vehicles and forcing residents to take detours or cross on foot.","n":[["2","Palestinians hit in the head by rubber-coated metal bullets","WAFA (local sources)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-he-irrigated-the-tree-with-his-blood-olive.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"06:00","r":"west-bank","k":"Story","h":"‘He irrigated the tree with his blood’: olive farmers brace for a violent harvest","g":"olive-harvest","x":"The National visits a Nablus-area grove where a father of four was killed by settlers on 7 October, and a family in Usarin still nursing its wounds.","s":"The National","sp":["The National"],"p":"Olive groves around Nablus, and the village of Usarin","b":"In the hills around Nablus, a 59-year-old farmer showed The National the olive tree where his cousin, a 44-year-old father of four, bled to death after settlers attacked the family’s grove on 7 October. He said the attackers fired at them, beat them with metal and wooden objects and threw stones; eight people were hurt, two by gunfire. Farther south in the village of Usarin, a 63-year-old woman described being beaten as she ran toward relatives ambushed by settlers in the grove beside their home. One of her sons was still in intensive care in Nablus with a severe brain injury, the paper reported. The National says the trees are full this year after a poor 2025 crop, that at least two Palestinians have been killed by settlers since farmers began returning to their groves in late September, and that both attacks it documented took place in Areas A and B, where the Palestinian Authority has full or partial control.","q":"He irrigated the tree with his blood.","w":"An olive farmer from the Nablus area, cousin of the man killed on 7 October, to The National","n":[["8","people injured in the 7 Oct settler attack on one family’s grove near Nablus, two by gunfire","The National"],["2","Palestinians killed by settlers since farmers returned to groves in late September","The National"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-germany-calls-gazas-humanitarian-situation-catastrophic-in-reply.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"08:31","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"Germany calls Gaza’s humanitarian situation catastrophic in reply to lawmakers","g":"humanitarian","x":"Berlin told the Left party’s parliamentary group that aid deliveries need comprehensive improvement and said it has given up to €420 million since October 2023.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Berlin, Germany","b":"The German government described the humanitarian situation in Gaza as catastrophic and said aid supplies need comprehensive improvement, in a reply on Tuesday to a parliamentary question from the Left party’s group in the Bundestag, WAFA reported. Berlin said its contacts with the relevant authorities cover customs procedures, restrictions on dual-use goods and the entry of specific items including petrol, engine oil and waterproof tents. Citing UN data, it said that although aid volumes rose after the October 2025 ceasefire, international NGOs’ access to Gaza had declined because of new Israeli restrictions. The government tied any lasting improvement to implementation of the Gaza peace plan, including rebuilding civilian infrastructure, and said Germany has allocated up to €420 million in humanitarian aid since 7 October 2023.","n":[["€420 million","German humanitarian aid allocated for Gaza since 7 Oct 2023 (up to)","German government (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-forces-arrest-seven-in-pre-dawn-raids-on.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"08:33","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces arrest seven in pre-dawn raids on Nablus and Qalqilya, WAFA says","g":"detention","x":"Troops also searched homes and questioned residents in Deir Dibwan, Burqa, Turmus Ayya and Qibya in Ramallah and al-Bireh governorate, security sources said.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Nablus","b":"Israeli forces arrested four Palestinians in Nablus before dawn on Wednesday after raiding the al-Ma’ajin and al-Dahiya neighbourhoods and the Askar and al-Ein refugee camps and searching homes, security and local sources told WAFA. In Qalqilya, troops entered from the city’s southern entrance, raided homes on al-Wad Street and arrested three men, among them a former prisoner, local sources said. Quds News Network put the number arrested in Qalqilya at five. In Ramallah and al-Bireh governorate, forces raided Deir Dibwan and Burqa to the east, Turmus Ayya to the north and Qibya to the west, searching homes and questioning their owners on the spot, security sources said (WAFA, 08:43).","n":[["4","Palestinians arrested in Nablus","WAFA (security and local sources)"],["3","Palestinians arrested in Qalqilya","WAFA (local sources)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-strike-on-gaza-city-apartment-kills-a.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"08:51","r":"gaza","k":"Update","h":"Israeli strike on Gaza City apartment kills a woman, local sources say","g":"killed-injured","x":"The apartment was near the Abu Talal junction in al-Rimal; several people were wounded, and heavy fire was reported east of Deir al-Balah and Khan Younis.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Abu Talal junction, al-Rimal, western Gaza City","b":"A woman was killed and others were injured when Israeli aircraft struck a residential apartment near the Abu Talal junction in Gaza City’s al-Rimal neighbourhood on Wednesday morning, local sources told WAFA. An explosion was heard alongside heavy Israeli artillery fire east of Khan Younis, and Israeli military vehicles fired heavily east of Deir al-Balah and south of Khan Younis, the sources said.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-settlers-set-fire-to-mosque-car-and-olive.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"09:00","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"Settlers set fire to mosque, car and olive tree in Mukhmas, governorate says","g":"settlers","x":"The attack came after midnight in the village northeast of Jerusalem; the Israeli military said no suspects were identified and it had opened an investigation.","s":"WAFA / Jerusalem Governorate; AFP","sp":["WAFA","Jerusalem Governorate","AFP"],"p":"Mukhmas, northeast of Jerusalem","b":"Israeli settlers entered Mukhmas, northeast of Jerusalem, after midnight and set fire to a mosque, an olive tree beside it and a car, which burned out completely, the Jerusalem Governorate said. AFP footage showed the fire had burned the mosque’s carpeting and blackened its walls, and Hebrew graffiti was sprayed on its exterior. The Israeli military said soldiers were sent after reports of arson and graffiti, that no suspects were identified and that an investigation had been opened, AFP reported. Palestinian National Council President Rouhi Fattouh condemned the attack and blamed international inaction for the continuing settler attacks, WAFA reported.","q":"a barbaric act and a dangerous escalation in settler terrorism","w":"Rouhi Fattouh, President, Palestinian National Council","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-forces-detain-eight-in-hebron-area-including.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"09:12","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces detain eight in Hebron area, including four boys, Prisoners’ Society says","g":"detention","x":"Detainees from Sa’ir, al-Burj and Beit Ula were beaten, the society said; a couple from al-Dhahiriya was questioned for hours in Kiryat Arba settlement.","s":"WAFA / Palestinian Prisoners' Society","sp":["WAFA","Palestinian Prisoners' Society"],"p":"Sa'ir, northeast of Hebron","b":"Israeli forces detained eight Palestinians in Hebron governorate on Wednesday, among them four boys aged 14 and 15 from the town of Sa’ir, the Palestinian Prisoners’ Society told WAFA. It said several of the detainees were beaten. Two men were taken from al-Burj, southwest of Dura, and a 62-year-old man from Beit Ula, west of Hebron. In al-Dhahiriya, a man and his wife were taken to Kiryat Arba settlement and questioned for hours before being released, the society said. Forces also set up a checkpoint in the Bir al-Hums area of central Hebron, stopping and searching residents and their cars, it said.","n":[["8","Palestinians detained in Hebron governorate","Palestinian Prisoners' Society (via WAFA)"],["4","boys aged 14-15 among them, all from Sa’ir","Palestinian Prisoners' Society (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-west-bank-settler-population-reaches-about-790000-statistics.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"09:28","r":"west-bank","k":"Figures","h":"West Bank settler population reaches about 790,000, statistics bureau says","g":"settlers","x":"A statement for Arab Environment Day says outposts set up in 2023-2025 control about 1.07 million dunums and displaced 118 herding communities.","s":"WAFA / PCBS and Environment Quality Authority","sp":["WAFA","PCBS and Environment Quality Authority"],"p":"West Bank","b":"About 790,000 settlers lived in the occupied West Bank at the end of July 2026, spread across 170 settlements and 400 outposts, the Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics and the Environment Quality Authority said in a joint statement for Arab Environment Day. Rangeland covers about 2,111 square kilometres, roughly 37 percent of the West Bank, the statement said. It said 185 new outposts set up between 2023 and 2025, about 130 of them herding outposts and hilltops, control around 1.07 million dunums, and that settler violence and blocked access to pasture and water displaced 118 herding communities over the same period. Separately, Amir Daoud of the Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission told Voice of Palestine radio that more than 110 Bedouin communities had been partly or fully displaced in the past three years.","n":[["790,000","settlers in the West Bank (170 settlements, 400 outposts)","Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics"],["37%","of the West Bank is rangeland (about 2,111 sq km)","PCBS and Environment Quality Authority"],["118","herding communities displaced, 2023-2025","PCBS and Environment Quality Authority"],["1.07 million","dunums controlled by 185 outposts set up in 2023-2025","PCBS and Environment Quality Authority"],["110+","Bedouin communities partly or fully displaced in three years","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (Amir Daoud)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-troops-tear-gas-jenin-area-olive-pickers-despite-prior-coordination.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"10:08","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Troops tear-gas Jenin-area olive pickers despite prior coordination, sources say","g":"olive-harvest","x":"Farmers from al-Fandaqumiya, Jaba’ and Silat al-Dhahr were forced out; in Ya’bad, settlers stole harvested olives and expelled pickers, local sources said.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"al-Fandaqumiya, south of Jenin","b":"Israeli forces fired tear gas at farmers from al-Fandaqumiya, Jaba’ and Silat al-Dhahr, south of Jenin, as they headed to their groves on Wednesday and forced them to leave, even though access had been coordinated in advance, local sources told WAFA. The sources said the army had turned the farmers away on Tuesday and told them to come back on Wednesday. Hundreds of families with land around Tarsala settlement were again unable to reach it. In Ya’bad, southwest of Jenin, settlers attacked pickers, stole their olives and forced them off land north of al-Sahl road, between the Kufeirit junction and Imreiha, after Palestinian liaison officials had told farmers that access was coordinated from 7 a.m., the sources said."},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-forces-detain-a-boy-in-issawiya-jerusalem.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"10:23","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces detain a boy in Issawiya, Jerusalem Governorate says","g":"detention","x":"Forces and Israeli municipal crews also entered the al-Bustan neighbourhood of Silwan, south of al-Aqsa Mosque, the governorate said.","s":"WAFA / Jerusalem Governorate","sp":["WAFA","Jerusalem Governorate"],"p":"Issawiya, East Jerusalem","b":"Israeli forces detained a boy after raiding his family’s home in Issawiya, in occupied East Jerusalem, before dawn on Wednesday, the Jerusalem Governorate said. His age was not given. Israeli forces and crews from the Israeli-run Jerusalem municipality also entered the al-Bustan neighbourhood of Silwan, south of al-Aqsa Mosque, the governorate said.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-gaza-consumer-prices-rose-1136-in-september-statistics.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"10:46","r":"gaza","k":"Figures","h":"Gaza consumer prices rose 11.36% in September, statistics bureau says","g":"humanitarian","x":"A 25-kilogram sack of white flour averaged 56 shekels in Gaza; prices rose 0.53% in the West Bank and 0.44% in Jerusalem, the bureau said.","s":"WAFA / Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics","sp":["WAFA","Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"Consumer prices in the Gaza Strip rose 11.36 percent in September from August, pushing the index for Palestine as a whole up 4.69 percent, the Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics said. Prices rose 0.53 percent in the West Bank and 0.44 percent in Jerusalem. Average prices in Gaza included 56 shekels for a 25-kilogram sack of white flour, 41 shekels for a kilogram of frozen veal and 661 shekels for a 20-cigarette pack of Royal cigarettes, the bureau said. Compared with September 2025, prices in Gaza were 46.43 percent lower, while West Bank prices were 3.34 percent higher, according to the bureau.","n":[["11.36%","rise in Gaza consumer prices, September vs August 2026","Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics"],["56","shekels, average price of a 25 kg sack of white flour in Gaza","Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-forces-demolish-home-and-family-shelter-in.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"10:50","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces demolish home and family shelter in Masafer Yatta, sources say","g":"demolition","x":"An 80-square-metre house in Ighzaywa and a 40-square-metre shelter for a family of seven in Umm al-Khair were razed; a mobile toilet was seized.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Umm al-Khair and Ighzaywa, Masafer Yatta, south of Hebron","b":"Israeli forces demolished an 80-square-metre house in Khirbet Ighzaywa, south of Yatta, and a 40-square-metre dwelling that housed a family of seven in Khirbet Umm al-Khair, in the Masafer Yatta area south of Hebron, local sources told WAFA. They also seized a mobile toilet. UN OCHA reported on 2 October that about 400 structures had been demolished in the Masafer Yatta communities located inside the area Israel designates Firing Zone 918, displacing 969 Palestinians.","n":[["2","structures demolished: an 80 sq m house and a 40 sq m shelter for a family of seven","WAFA (local sources)"],["969","Palestinians displaced by about 400 demolitions in Masafer Yatta’s Firing Zone 918 communities","UN OCHA"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-settlers-bulldoze-land-in-beit-dajan-and-burin.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"11:00","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers bulldoze land in Beit Dajan and Burin to prepare new outpost, sources say","g":"settlers","x":"Two bulldozers opened new roads east of Beit Dajan and razed land around a family’s home in Burin, local and security sources told WAFA.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Burin, south of Nablus","b":"Settlers accompanied by two bulldozers razed Palestinian land in Beit Dajan, east of Nablus, and Burin, to the south, on Wednesday morning, local and security sources told WAFA. In Beit Dajan the settlers began opening new roads on the town’s eastern side; in Burin they razed land around a family’s home to prepare for a new outpost, the sources said."},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-settlers-try-to-stop-olive-harvest-in-turmus.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"11:27","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers try to stop olive harvest in Turmus Ayya; residents push them back, sources say","g":"olive-harvest","x":"The settlers retreated toward an outpost set up on the town’s plain late last year, local sources told WAFA.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Turmus Ayya, north of Ramallah","b":"A group of settlers attacked olive pickers in the northeast of Turmus Ayya, north of Ramallah, on Wednesday morning and tried to stop the harvest, local sources told WAFA. Residents confronted them and forced them back toward the outpost settlers set up on the town’s plain. According to WAFA, settlers established that outpost at the end of last year, and Israeli bulldozers uprooted more than 2,000 olive trees to open a road linking it to Shilo settlement, which is built on the town’s land. The Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission counted 219 settler attacks from 1 to 12 October in a preliminary tally based on field reports, 136 of them linked to the olive harvest, WAFA reported.","n":[["2,000+","olive trees uprooted to build a road from the outpost to Shilo settlement","WAFA"],["136","of 219 attacks recorded 1-12 Oct linked to the olive harvest (preliminary)","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-settlers-beat-17-year-old-picking-olives-in-beita-red.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"11:31","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers beat 17-year-old picking olives in Beita, Red Crescent says","g":"olive-harvest","x":"In nearby Beit Furik, settlers stopped farmers harvesting east of al-Naqra road, in Area B, and dumped the olives they had gathered, local sources said.","s":"WAFA / Palestine Red Crescent Society","sp":["WAFA","Palestine Red Crescent Society"],"p":"Beita, south of Nablus","b":"A 17-year-old was injured when settlers beat him while he and other farmers were picking olives in Beita, south of Nablus, the Palestine Red Crescent Society said. Local sources told WAFA that settlers attacked the pickers and beat them. In Beit Furik, east of Nablus, settlers stopped residents from harvesting east of al-Naqra road, an area classified as Area B, and threw the olives they had collected onto the ground, local sources told WAFA.","n":[["17","age of the boy injured in the Beita attack","Palestine Red Crescent Society (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-amnesty-germany-gives-2026-human-rights-award-to.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"12:02","r":"prisoners","k":"Story","h":"Amnesty Germany gives 2026 human rights award to children’s lawyer Khaled Quzmar","g":"prisoners","x":"The Ramallah-based lawyer has defended Palestinian children for nearly 40 years; the prize will be presented in Berlin on 25 November, WAFA reported.","s":"Amnesty International Germany / WAFA","sp":["Amnesty International Germany","WAFA"],"p":"Ramallah","b":"Amnesty International’s German section has chosen Khaled Quzmar, a Ramallah-based lawyer who has spent nearly four decades defending Palestinian children, for its 2026 Human Rights Award. WAFA reported the award on Wednesday. Amnesty said Quzmar documented violations of children’s rights in Gaza and the West Bank, including East Jerusalem, for UN bodies and international courts, and was summoned by Israeli intelligence in 2021 and threatened with a long prison term. As long-time director general of Defense for Children International - Palestine, he dissolved the organisation in spring 2026 after more than 35 years because of continuing harassment, Amnesty said. The award, worth €20,000, is given every two years; this is the 13th. WAFA said it will be presented in Berlin on 25 November 2026. Amnesty says at least 351 minors are currently held in Israeli prisons, more than half of them in administrative detention.","q":"Palestinian children have the least influence on the conflict and often have to pay the highest price.","w":"Khaled Quzmar, Palestinian children's rights lawyer, 2026 Amnesty Germany Human Rights Award recipient","n":[["351","Palestinian minors held in Israeli prisons (at least), more than half in administrative detention","Amnesty International Germany"],["€20,000","value of Amnesty Germany’s Human Rights Award","Amnesty International Germany"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-gaza-death-toll-since-october-2023-reaches-74392.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"12:06","r":"gaza","k":"Figures","h":"Gaza death toll since October 2023 reaches 74,392, medical sources say","g":"killed-injured","x":"Three bodies and 17 injured people reached hospitals in 24 hours; 1,501 people have been killed since the ceasefire began a year ago.","s":"WAFA / Gaza medical sources","sp":["WAFA","Gaza medical sources"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"The number of Palestinians killed in Gaza since 7 October 2023 has risen to 74,392, with 175,440 injured, medical sources said on Wednesday. Three bodies, including that of a person who died of earlier wounds, and 17 injured people reached hospitals in the previous 24 hours, the sources said. Since the ceasefire took effect on 11 October 2025, 1,501 people have been killed and 5,323 injured, and the bodies of 838 people have been recovered, according to the same sources.","n":[["74,392","Palestinians killed in Gaza since 7 Oct 2023","Gaza medical sources (via WAFA)"],["175,440","injured in Gaza since 7 Oct 2023","Gaza medical sources (via WAFA)"],["1,501","killed since the 11 Oct 2025 ceasefire","Gaza medical sources (via WAFA)"],["838","bodies recovered since the ceasefire","Gaza medical sources (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-bulldozers-uproot-hundreds-of-olive-trees-in.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"12:14","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli bulldozers uproot hundreds of olive trees in Kafr Malik, sources say","g":"olive-harvest","x":"Thirty-five dunums of farmland beside the bypass road were razed under a military order issued ten days earlier, local sources told WAFA.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Kafr Malik, east of Ramallah","b":"Israeli bulldozers began razing 35 dunums of farmland in the southern part of Kafr Malik, east of Ramallah, early on Wednesday, uprooting hundreds of fruit-bearing olive trees along the bypass road, local sources told WAFA. The sources said Israeli authorities had issued a military order ten days earlier to raze dozens of dunums and uproot hundreds of trees in the area, where 56 dunums planted with hundreds of trees were razed several months ago.","n":[["35","dunums of farmland razed","WAFA (local sources)"],["56","dunums razed in the same area several months earlier","WAFA (local sources)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-more-than-430-jerusalem-homes-and-structures-demolished.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"12:24","r":"jerusalem","k":"Figures","h":"More than 430 Jerusalem homes and structures demolished this year, researcher says","g":"demolition","x":"Fakhri Abu Diab told Voice of Palestine radio that 23,280 demolition orders are in force and that no more than 1% of Jerusalemites who applied this year got permits.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"East Jerusalem","b":"Israeli authorities have demolished more than 430 homes and other structures in occupied East Jerusalem since the start of 2026, Jerusalem affairs researcher Fakhri Abu Diab told Voice of Palestine radio. He said 23,280 administrative and judicial demolition orders are in force in the city. Beit Hanina, al-Tur, Silwan and Jabal al-Mukabbir are among the most targeted neighbourhoods, Abu Diab said. He estimated that about 33 percent of Palestinian homes in Jerusalem are under threat of demolition and put the number demolished since 1967 at about 37,000. More than 1,200 Jerusalemites applied for building permits this year despite meeting the conditions, he said, and no more than 1 percent have received one.","n":[["430+","homes and structures demolished in East Jerusalem since January 2026","Fakhri Abu Diab (via WAFA)"],["23,280","administrative and judicial demolition orders in force","Fakhri Abu Diab (via WAFA)"],["33%","of Palestinian homes in Jerusalem under threat of demolition (estimate)","Fakhri Abu Diab (via WAFA)"],["1%","of 1,200+ permit applicants this year who received a building permit (at most)","Fakhri Abu Diab (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-two-women-held-in-damon-prison-have-scabies.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"12:36","r":"prisoners","k":"Update","h":"Two women held in Damon prison have scabies, prisoners’ commission says","g":"prisoners","x":"The detainees, aged 20 and 31, told a commission lawyer their rooms were raided with gas and sound grenades on 7 October and that food is scarce.","s":"WAFA / Commission of Detainees' and Ex-Detainees' Affairs","sp":["WAFA","Commission of Detainees' and Ex-Detainees' Affairs"],"p":"Damon prison, near Haifa","b":"Two Palestinian women held in Israel’s Damon prison, aged 20 and 31, have scabies made worse by a lack of hygiene supplies and high humidity, the Commission of Detainees’ and Ex-Detainees’ Affairs said. One, held since 13 March 2026, told a commission lawyer the infection caused blisters and swelling on her legs, that medication had not helped, and that she was put in solitary confinement after her room was raided on 7 October. The other, held since 27 February, described constant itching that keeps her awake at night. Both said their rooms had been raided repeatedly, including with gas and sound grenades on 7 October, and that food was scarce and of poor quality, the commission said.","n":[["2","women detainees with scabies in Damon prison","Commission of Detainees' and Ex-Detainees' Affairs (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-egyptian-surgeons-resume-open-heart-operations-at-gaza-city.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"12:44","r":"gaza","k":"Story","h":"Egyptian surgeons resume open-heart operations at Gaza City hospital","g":"humanitarian","x":"The team at al-Quds Hospital, working with HEAL Palestine, aims to operate on about 30 patients while more than 200 critical cases wait, WAFA reported.","s":"WAFA (video report)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"al-Quds Hospital, Tal al-Hawa, Gaza City","b":"A specialised Egyptian medical team is carrying out open-heart surgery at al-Quds Hospital in Gaza City after such operations resumed in cooperation with the charity HEAL Palestine, according to a WAFA video report. The mission aims to operate on about 30 patients, while more than 200 critical cases remain on waiting lists, WAFA said.","n":[["30","patients the Egyptian team aims to operate on","WAFA"],["200+","critical cardiac cases on waiting lists","WAFA"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-gaza-health-ministry-says-68-of-116-hospital.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"13:03","r":"gaza","k":"Figures","h":"Gaza health ministry says 68 of 116 hospital generators are out of service","g":"humanitarian","x":"Neonatal care at al-Rantisi and al-Shifa has been disrupted; a medical relief director says 2,027 patients have died awaiting treatment or evacuation.","s":"Quds News Network / WAFA","sp":["Quds News Network","WAFA"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"Sixty-eight of the 116 generators serving Gaza’s health facilities are out of service, 44 are still running under constant strain and eight more need urgent repairs that cannot be done for lack of spare parts, Mazen al-Arayshi, director general of engineering and maintenance at the Gaza Health Ministry, told Quds News Network. The shortages have disrupted incubator and premature-baby services at al-Rantisi Hospital and al-Shifa Medical Complex, and radiology, laboratory and dental services at clinics in al-Maghazi, al-Zawaida and al-Fallah, he said. Earlier, Mohammad Abu Afash, director of Palestinian Medical Relief in Gaza, told Voice of Palestine radio that 2,027 patients had died while waiting for treatment or medical evacuation, that more than 21,000 still need evacuation and that no patient had left through the Rafah crossing for more than five days. Only 227 of more than 574 pre-war health centres are operating, with limited means, he said.","n":[["68 of 116","generators at Gaza health facilities out of service","Gaza Health Ministry (Mazen al-Arayshi, via Quds News Network)"],["2,027","patients who died waiting for treatment or medical evacuation","Palestinian Medical Relief (Mohammad Abu Afash, via WAFA)"],["21,000+","patients awaiting medical evacuation","Palestinian Medical Relief (via WAFA)"],["227","of 574+ pre-war health centres still operating","Palestinian Medical Relief (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-tuqu-sealed-for-a-second-day-as-israeli.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"13:09","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Tuqu’ sealed for a second day as Israeli forces install new gate near Jenin","g":"checkpoint","x":"All seven entrances to Tuqu’ are closed with iron gates, its mayor said; a new gate at Wad Da’uq follows gates fitted at Arraba’s entrances on Tuesday.","s":"WAFA / Tuqu' Municipality","sp":["WAFA","Tuqu' Municipality"],"p":"Tuqu', southeast of Bethlehem","b":"Israeli forces kept all seven entrances to Tuqu’, southeast of Bethlehem, shut with iron gates for a second straight day and sealed other roads with earth mounds, isolating the town and keeping residents from work, Mayor Saqr Suleiman told WAFA. The town’s entrances have been closed repeatedly; at the end of September forces closed three main entrances linking Tuqu’ to Bethlehem, WAFA reported. In Jenin governorate, Israeli forces began installing an iron gate at the main entrance of Wad Da’uq on Wednesday and closed the road to traffic, local sources said, a day after gates were fitted at the entrances of nearby Arraba.","n":[["7","entrances to Tuqu’ closed with iron gates","Tuqu' Municipality (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-colombia-opens-embassy-in-jerusalem-palestinian-ministry-calls.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"13:18","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"Colombia opens embassy in Jerusalem; Palestinian ministry calls it a violation","g":"diplomacy","x":"Colombia is the tenth country with an embassy in the city, Reuters reported; Ramallah says the move breaches Security Council resolutions 476 and 478.","s":"WAFA (Palestinian Foreign Ministry); Reuters","sp":["WAFA","Reuters"],"p":"Jerusalem","b":"Colombia opened its embassy to Israel in Jerusalem on Wednesday at a ceremony attended by Israeli Foreign Minister Gideon Sa’ar and Colombian Foreign Minister Omar Bula Escobar, making it the tenth country with an embassy in the city, Reuters reported. Former president Gustavo Petro broke off relations with Israel in May 2024; his successor, Abelardo de la Espriella, restored them, and a free trade agreement was reinstated on Tuesday. The Palestinian Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Expatriates condemned the opening as a violation of international law and of Security Council resolutions 476 and 478, urged Bogotá to reverse it and called on states to take political, diplomatic, economic and legal steps against any country that opens or moves an embassy to Jerusalem. Palestine’s envoy to the Arab League, Muhannad al-Aklouk, told Voice of Palestine radio that Arab states had decided to summon Colombia’s ambassadors and had asked members of the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation to do the same.","n":[["10","countries with an embassy in Jerusalem after Colombia’s opening","Reuters (via The Jerusalem Post)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-more-than-2000-children-detained-since-october-2023.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"13:26","r":"prisoners","k":"Figures","h":"More than 2,000 children detained since October 2023, Prisoners’ Society says","g":"prisoners","x":"At least 350 remain in custody, the society said, after at least six children were detained overnight in Sa’ir and al-Shuyukh, near Hebron.","s":"Quds News Network / Palestinian Prisoners' Society","sp":["Quds News Network","Palestinian Prisoners' Society"],"p":"West Bank and Israeli prisons","b":"Israeli authorities have detained more than 2,000 Palestinian children since October 2023, and at least 350 remain in custody, the Palestinian Prisoners’ Society said in a statement reported by Quds News Network. The count does not include children from Gaza, whose number is unclear because of enforced disappearances, the society said. At least six children were detained in the West Bank overnight, most of them in Sa’ir and al-Shuyukh in Hebron governorate, it said. Society head Abdullah al-Zaghari told Voice of Palestine radio that about 9,400 Palestinians are held in Israeli prisons, where he said torture, starvation and medical neglect have continued for nearly three years and Red Cross visits are barred. Prisoner groups’ report of 6 October counted more than 26,000 arrests in the West Bank since the war began.","n":[["2,000+","Palestinian children detained since October 2023 (excluding Gaza)","Palestinian Prisoners' Society (via Quds News Network)"],["350","children still held (at least)","Palestinian Prisoners' Society (via Quds News Network)"],["9,400","Palestinians held in Israeli prisons (approx.)","Palestinian Prisoners' Society (Abdullah al-Zaghari, via WAFA)"],["26,000+","arrests in the West Bank since October 2023","Palestinian prisoners' institutions"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-forces-ransack-prisoners-commission-office-in-salfit.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"13:52","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces ransack prisoners’ commission office in Salfit, demolish Haris workshops","g":"raid","x":"Troops tore photographs and questioned staff at the office, its director said; three vehicle-repair shops owned by two brothers were razed in Haris.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Salfit and Haris, Salfit governorate","b":"Israeli forces raided the Salfit office of the Commission of Detainees’ and Ex-Detainees’ Affairs on Wednesday, scattering its contents, tearing photographs inside and questioning staff on the spot, the office director told WAFA. Troops also took up positions on a rooftop in the city and blocked traffic, local sources said. In Haris, west of Salfit, Israeli forces demolished three vehicle-repair and bodywork shops near the village traffic lights belonging to two brothers, measuring about 120, 170 and 150 square metres, local sources said. The report gave no reason for the demolition.","n":[["3","vehicle-repair shops demolished in Haris (about 120, 170 and 150 sq m)","WAFA (local sources)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-forces-detain-mother-of-man-they-killed.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"13:53","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces detain mother of man they killed in 2023, near Tulkarm checkpoint","g":"detention","x":"The 47-year-old woman from Tulkarm refugee camp was detained near Jubara checkpoint, the Prisoners’ Society said; two young men were also arrested in the governorate.","s":"WAFA / Palestinian Prisoners' Society","sp":["WAFA","Palestinian Prisoners' Society"],"p":"Tulkarm refugee camp","b":"Israeli forces detained a 47-year-old woman from Tulkarm refugee camp near the Jubara checkpoint, south of the city, on Wednesday, the Palestinian Prisoners’ Society in Tulkarm told WAFA. She is the mother of a man killed by Israeli forces in the city’s eastern neighbourhood on 6 November 2023, the society said. Earlier, forces arrested a young man in Tulkarm’s southern neighbourhood and another in the town of Illar to the north, and searched two homes in Izbat Shufa, local sources said.","n":[["47","age of the woman detained near Jubara checkpoint","Palestinian Prisoners' Society (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-man-90-and-woman-beaten-during-hours-long-israeli.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"14:00","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Man, 90, and woman beaten during hours-long Israeli raid on Tammun, Red Crescent says","g":"raid","x":"Special forces surrounded a house in the town south of Tubas; a young man was detained to pressure his brother to surrender, the Prisoners’ Society said.","s":"WAFA / Palestine Red Crescent Society","sp":["WAFA","Palestine Red Crescent Society"],"p":"Tammun, south of Tubas","b":"Palestine Red Crescent crews treated a 90-year-old man and a 34-year-old woman who were beaten by Israeli forces during a raid on Tammun, south of Tubas, medical sources told WAFA. Israeli special forces slipped into the town in the morning and surrounded a house, followed by military reinforcements, with reconnaissance aircraft overhead, said Kamal Bani Odeh, the Prisoners’ Society’s director in Tubas. He said troops occupied the family home for hours and detained a young man to pressure his brother to turn himself in. In a later report, Quds News Network gave a different account, saying forces arrested a man Israel had long been pursuing and wounded his son.","n":[["90","age of the man beaten during the raid","Palestine Red Crescent Society (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-traders-return-to-khan-youniss-historic-grain-market.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"14:25","r":"gaza","k":"Story","h":"Traders return to Khan Younis’s historic grain market","g":"humanitarian","x":"A WAFA video shows vendors back at work in the old market in southern Gaza as prices in the Strip keep climbing.","s":"WAFA (video report)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Khan Younis, southern Gaza Strip","b":"Traders have resumed work in the historic grain market in Khan Younis, in the southern Gaza Strip, according to a WAFA video report published on Wednesday. The Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics said the same day that consumer prices in Gaza rose 11.36 percent in September from August, with a 25-kilogram sack of white flour averaging 56 shekels.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-displaced-families-living-in-a-khan-younis-cemetery.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"15:06","r":"gaza","k":"Story","h":"Displaced families living in a Khan Younis cemetery patch tents before winter","g":"humanitarian","x":"A WAFA video shows families covering worn tents with nylon and tarpaulins against the rain, with no safe shelter as the wet season nears.","s":"WAFA (video report)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Cemetery in Khan Younis, southern Gaza Strip","b":"Displaced Palestinian families are living in tents inside a cemetery in Khan Younis, in the southern Gaza Strip, and covering them with nylon sheeting and tarpaulins to keep out rainwater, according to a WAFA video report. WAFA said the families face harsh living conditions and have no safe shelter as winter approaches. Another WAFA video on Wednesday showed displaced people in a camp in central Gaza City tying down their worn tents with ropes. The International Organization for Migration said on 9 October that almost 2 million people in Gaza need shelter assistance and that shelter partners’ supplies cover barely 17 percent of identified winter needs, according to Security Council Report.","n":[["17%","of identified winter shelter needs covered by partners’ supply pipeline","IOM (via Security Council Report)"],["~2 million","people in Gaza needing shelter assistance","IOM (via Security Council Report)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-palestinian-central-bank-chief-urges-imf-backed-fix-for.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"15:47","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"Palestinian central bank chief urges IMF-backed fix for banking ties with Israel","g":"diplomacy","x":"Yahya Shunnar said about 50 billion shekels a year flows through correspondent banking with Israeli banks, whose legal cover has been cut to two-week periods.","s":"WAFA / Palestine Monetary Authority","sp":["WAFA","Palestine Monetary Authority"],"p":"Bangkok, Thailand","b":"Palestine Monetary Authority Governor Yahya Shunnar asked the International Monetary Fund to help find a lasting solution, under neutral international sponsorship, for correspondent banking relations with Israeli banks, so that payments and the return of shekel cash to Israel continue regardless of politics. Speaking in Bangkok at a meeting of finance ministers and central bank governors from the Middle East, North Africa, Afghanistan and Pakistan with IMF Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva, Shunnar said transactions worth about 50 billion shekels a year pass through those relationships, covering payments for food, fuel, medicine, salaries, customs and aid. He warned that shortening the legal cover for the relationships to two weeks at a time deepens uncertainty, alongside limits on returning shekel cash and Israel’s continued withholding of clearance revenues, and said Palestinian banks’ capital adequacy ratio exceeds 17 percent.","n":[["50 billion","shekels a year passing through correspondent banking with Israeli banks (approx.)","Palestine Monetary Authority (Yahya Shunnar, via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-plo-refugee-official-warns-unrwa-against-laying-off.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"16:12","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"PLO refugee official warns UNRWA against laying off staff over budget gap","g":"humanitarian","x":"Ahmad Abu Holi wrote to Commissioner-General Christian Saunders after Saunders said in Berlin that large layoffs were possible without new funding.","s":"WAFA / PLO Department of Refugee Affairs","sp":["WAFA","PLO Department of Refugee Affairs"],"p":"Ramallah","b":"Ahmad Abu Holi, a PLO Executive Committee member who heads its Department of Refugee Affairs, warned UNRWA against laying off or cutting staff because of the deficit in its regular budget, saying it would threaten the agency’s stability and its ability to serve Palestinian refugees. In a letter to UNRWA Commissioner-General Christian Saunders, Abu Holi voiced concern at remarks Saunders made at the Diplomatic Academy in Berlin about possible large-scale layoffs if additional funding does not arrive soon. He rejected a smaller agency built on staff cuts and proposed temporary alternatives, including deferring salary payments where necessary, until funding is secured. Closing the funding gap is the international community’s responsibility and must not come at the expense of staff or refugees, Abu Holi said."},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-arab-league-to-mark-its-first-remembrance-day.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"17:15","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"Arab League to mark its first remembrance day for what it calls genocide victims","g":"diplomacy","x":"Arab ministers designated 17 October each year; the League says more than 700 people were killed in Gaza on that day in 2023, including at al-Ahli hospital.","s":"WAFA / Arab League","sp":["WAFA","Arab League"],"p":"Arab League headquarters, Cairo","b":"The Arab League secretariat will hold a commemoration at its Cairo headquarters on Sunday for victims of what it calls the genocide committed by Israel against the Palestinian people, especially in Gaza, the League said in a statement. It is the first time the day will be marked at the League. Arab foreign ministers adopted 17 October as an annual day of remembrance at their 166th regular session in September 2026, the League said. It said more than 700 people were killed on 17 October 2023 in under 24 hours, including in the massacre at al-Ahli Arab (Baptist) Hospital in Gaza City.","n":[["700+","people killed in Gaza on 17 Oct 2023 in under 24 hours","Arab League (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israels-army-chief-warns-of-potential-for-escalation.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"17:25","r":"west-bank","k":"Statement","h":"Israel’s army chief warns of ‘potential for escalation,’ condemns settler violence","g":"settlers","x":"Lt. Gen. Eyal Zamir said the army would keep enabling the olive harvest; the 98th Division has been sent to the West Bank, The Times of Israel reported.","s":"The Times of Israel","sp":["The Times of Israel"],"p":"Occupied West Bank","b":"Israeli military chief of staff Lt. Gen. Eyal Zamir said numerous alerts on all fronts had increased the potential for escalation, during a visit to Central Command headquarters with its head, Maj. Gen. Avi Bluth, The Times of Israel reported. The 98th Division has been deployed to the West Bank to boost the army’s presence there, the paper said. Zamir said the army was ready to move to broad offensive operations against anyone who tries to harm Israeli civilians, and would keep enabling the olive harvest while maintaining public order. He said violence against Palestinians must be eradicated using every tool available to the state, according to the report. Quds News Network, citing an army statement, reported that Zamir approved plans to continue military operations in the West Bank.","q":"Harming and committing violence against innocent people does not reflect the Israeli spirit or the Jewish spirit.","w":"Eyal Zamir, Chief of Staff, Israeli military"},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-troops-raid-red-crescent-office-and-hospital.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"17:47","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli troops raid Red Crescent office and hospital in Salfit, seize camera footage","g":"raid","x":"Soldiers threatened to open fire if the Yasser Arafat Hospital did not hand over its recordings, official sources told WAFA; troops came back later in the day.","s":"WAFA (official sources)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Martyr Yasser Arafat Hospital, Salfit","b":"Israeli forces raided the Palestine Red Crescent Society headquarters and the Martyr Yasser Arafat Hospital in Salfit on Wednesday, official sources told WAFA. They took surveillance camera recordings from the Red Crescent office and demanded that the hospital’s management hand over its camera footage. The troops threatened to open fire if the recordings were not handed over, then pulled back to the hospital entrance, the sources said. WAFA’s correspondent said forces raided the hospital a second time later in the day.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-arab-league-oic-and-egypt-condemn-colombias-jerusalem.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"18:15","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"Arab League, OIC and Egypt condemn Colombia’s Jerusalem embassy","g":"diplomacy","x":"The League cited Security Council resolutions 478 and 2334; the OIC chief wrote to Colombia’s foreign minister urging a reversal and warned of consequences.","s":"WAFA (Arab League, OIC, Egyptian Foreign Ministry statements)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Cairo","b":"The Arab League condemned Colombia’s opening of an embassy in Jerusalem as contrary to international resolutions, citing Security Council Resolution 478 of 1980, which called on states to withdraw diplomatic missions from the city, and Resolution 2334 of 2016. It said the step came despite repeated warnings and contacts with Bogotá and urged Colombia to reverse it. Organisation of Islamic Cooperation Secretary-General Ismail Ould Cheikh Ahmed said the move violates international law and Resolution 478, wrote to Colombia’s foreign minister urging him to reverse it and warned of far-reaching consequences for Colombia’s relations with the Muslim world, WAFA reported. Egypt’s Foreign Ministry also rejected the step, calling unilateral measures to change Jerusalem’s legal and historical status null and void.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-ministry-condemns-mukhmas-mosque-arson-as-commission-counts.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"18:41","r":"jerusalem","k":"Statement","h":"Ministry condemns Mukhmas mosque arson as commission counts 23 since 2023","g":"settlers","x":"Racist slogans were sprayed on the Sultan Ibrahim Mosque, the ministry said; the commission counts 13 arson attacks on mosques so far in 2026.","s":"WAFA / Palestinian Foreign Ministry; Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission","sp":["WAFA","Palestinian Foreign Ministry","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission"],"p":"Mukhmas, northeast of Jerusalem","b":"The Palestinian Ministry of Foreign Affairs condemned the burning of the Sultan Ibrahim Mosque in Mukhmas, northeast of Jerusalem, before dawn on Wednesday, saying settlers also burned residents’ property and olive trees and sprayed racist and threatening slogans on the mosque walls. The ministry urged the UN and its agencies to take binding measures to protect Palestinians and their holy sites, and to designate armed settler groups as terrorist organisations and sanction their members and backers. Earlier in the day, the Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission said it had documented 23 arson attacks or attempted arson attacks on Palestinian mosques and their facilities from January 2023 to 11 October 2026, 20 of them by settlers and 3 attributed to the Israeli army. Thirteen took place in 2026, double the total for 2025, it said.","n":[["23","arson attacks or attempts on mosques, Jan 2023 - 11 Oct 2026","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"],["13","of them in 2026, double the 6 recorded in 2025","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-settlers-beat-three-palestinians-in-abu-nujeim-southeast.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"19:00","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers beat three Palestinians in Abu Nujeim, southeast of Bethlehem, WAFA reports","g":"settlers","x":"One of the men was taken to hospital; the area has faced repeated attacks, including gunfire at residents’ cars in August, WAFA said.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Abu Nujeim, southeast of Bethlehem","b":"A group of settlers stormed the Abu Nujeim area southeast of Bethlehem on Wednesday and beat three Palestinians, one of whom was taken to hospital for treatment, WAFA’s correspondent reported. Abu Nujeim has faced repeated attacks on residents, their homes and property, WAFA said. On 7 August settlers fired live ammunition at residents’ cars there and assaulted ambulance crews, and there have been attempts to set up an outpost on the village’s land, the agency said.","n":[["3","Palestinians beaten by settlers","WAFA"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-vice-president-al-sheikh-condemns-colombias-embassy-opening-in.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"19:04","r":"jerusalem","k":"Statement","h":"Vice President al-Sheikh condemns Colombia’s embassy opening in Jerusalem","g":"diplomacy","x":"Hussein al-Sheikh said the move flouts international law and the city’s special legal and historical status, and urged the world to act.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Ramallah","b":"Palestinian Vice President Hussein al-Sheikh condemned the Colombian government’s opening of an embassy in Jerusalem, which he called the capital of the State of Palestine within the 1967 borders. In a post on X, he said the step flagrantly disregards international law and violates the special legal and historical status of the holy city, and called on the international community to confront measures aimed at changing Jerusalem’s identity and status, WAFA reported.","q":"We condemn the Colombian government’s opening of an “embassy” in occupied Jerusalem, the capital of the State of Palestine within the 1967 borders.","w":"Hussein al-Sheikh, Vice President of the State of Palestine","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-settlers-attack-qawawis-homes-in-masafer-yatta-elderly.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"19:13","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers attack Qawawis homes in Masafer Yatta; elderly man hurt, owner arrested","g":"settlers","x":"Settlers smashed doors and cameras before Israeli forces arrived and detained the owner of the damaged house; olives were stolen east of Yatta, an activist said.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Khirbet Qawawis, Masafer Yatta, south of Hebron","b":"Settlers attacked homes in Khirbet Qawawis in Masafer Yatta, south of Hebron, on Wednesday, bruising an elderly man who was taken by ambulance to Yatta’s government hospital, a local activist told WAFA. The settlers smashed a house’s doors, surveillance cameras and other property; Israeli forces then entered the area and arrested the owner of the damaged house, the activist said. In the Ighzaywa and Wadi Ma’in areas east of Yatta, settlers stole large quantities of olives from two families’ land and broke into an agricultural room, taking tools. Residents say attacks have increased since settlers set up a new outpost a few dozen metres from their homes about four months ago, WAFA reported.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-three-killed-in-israeli-strikes-and-fire-in.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"19:24","r":"gaza","k":"Update","h":"Three killed in Israeli strikes and fire in Gaza, bringing day’s toll to five, WAFA says","g":"killed-injured","x":"A drone strike on a car in al-Qarara, north of Khan Younis, killed two men and wounded a woman; another man was killed by gunfire in Jabalia camp.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"al-Qarara, north of Khan Younis; Jabalia camp","b":"An Israeli drone struck a vehicle on al-Hayya Street in al-Qarara, north of Khan Younis, on Wednesday evening, setting it on fire and killing the two men inside, WAFA’s correspondent said. A woman was injured; all were taken to Nasser Medical Complex. Earlier, a man was killed and others were injured by Israeli fire in Jabalia refugee camp in northern Gaza, WAFA said, putting the number killed in Gaza since dawn at five. Israeli artillery south of Khan Younis shelled western Rafah, and Israeli forces carried out demolitions east of Gaza City and Khan Younis, the agency reported.","n":[["3","Palestinians killed in the evening strikes and gunfire","WAFA"],["5","killed in Gaza since dawn, per this report (WAFA’s late round-up lists four)","WAFA"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-commission-records-21-attacks-on-olive-harvest-across.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"19:34","r":"west-bank","k":"Figures","h":"Commission records 21 attacks on olive harvest across seven governorates in one day","g":"olive-harvest","x":"Settlers carried out 12, the army 8 and one was joint, between 6 a.m. and 6 p.m., the Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission said.","s":"WAFA / Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission","sp":["WAFA","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission"],"p":"West Bank","b":"Israeli forces and settlers carried out 21 attacks on farmers, their land and the olive crop in seven governorates between 6 a.m. and 6 p.m. on Wednesday, the Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission said in an evening report: 12 by settlers, 8 by the army and one joint attack. They included assaults, detentions, blocking the harvest, theft of olives and equipment, land razing and the uprooting and burning of trees, the commission said. In Beita, a farmer and a 17-year-old were beaten with clubs at Jabal Qmas; in Jinsafut, settlers smashed two electric olive-harvesting machines; in Idhna, troops and settlers forced farmers and activists off land threatened with seizure. The commission said the targeting of the season undermines farmers’ ability to stay on and use their land, and called for their protection and for those responsible to be held to account.","n":[["21","attacks on farmers, land and the olive crop, 06:00-18:00 on 14 Oct","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"],["12","by settlers (8 by the army, 1 joint)","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"],["7","governorates affected","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-journalists-syndicate-says-israel-is-waging-a-wide.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"20:49","r":"west-bank","k":"Statement","h":"Journalists’ syndicate says Israel is waging a wide war on Palestinian reporters","g":"killed-injured","x":"The syndicate said 260 journalists have been killed in Gaza and more than 240 detained, 38 of whom are still held, and asked for protection in the field.","s":"WAFA / Palestinian Journalists Syndicate","sp":["WAFA","Palestinian Journalists Syndicate"],"p":"Ramallah","b":"The Palestinian Journalists Syndicate warned that Israel is waging a wide-scale war against Palestinian journalists across the West Bank, including occupied East Jerusalem, and that armed settlers are increasingly targeting media crews. In Gaza, 260 journalists have been killed, more than 240 detained, 38 of whom remain in custody, and more than 150 media institutions fully or partly destroyed, the syndicate said. On Tuesday Israeli forces held four journalists covering settler attacks on olive pickers in Idhna, west of Hebron, WAFA reported. With the International Day to End Impunity for Crimes against Journalists approaching, the syndicate called on the International Federation of Journalists, the UN and rights groups to provide field protection for journalists and hold those responsible to account.","n":[["260","journalists killed in Gaza since October 2023","Palestinian Journalists Syndicate (via WAFA)"],["38","journalists still held, of more than 240 detained","Palestinian Journalists Syndicate (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-jordan-and-australia-reaffirm-two-state-solution-reject-west.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"21:15","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"Jordan and Australia reaffirm two-state solution, reject West Bank annexation","g":"diplomacy","x":"A joint statement after King Abdullah II’s talks with Prime Minister Anthony Albanese in Canberra also condemned the E1 settlement project as illegal.","s":"WAFA (Jordan-Australia joint statement)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Canberra, Australia","b":"Jordan and Australia reaffirmed their commitment to a two-state solution and rejected any attempt to annex the West Bank or displace Palestinians, in a joint statement issued after talks between King Abdullah II and Prime Minister Anthony Albanese in Canberra. The statement condemned the E1 settlement project as illegal, stressed the historical and legal status quo at Jerusalem’s holy sites and the Hashemite custodianship, and called for full implementation of the Gaza peace plan under Security Council Resolution 2803 (2025) and for support to UNRWA, WAFA reported."},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-un-says-it-recorded-israeli-air-strikes-shelling.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"21:48","r":"gaza","k":"Statement","h":"UN says it recorded Israeli air strikes, shelling and gunfire across all of Gaza","g":"humanitarian","x":"Deputy spokesperson Farhan Haq said the aid the UN can bring in falls short of needs and that access is restricted to more than 64% of Gaza’s land.","s":"WAFA (UN noon briefing)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"UN safety and security staff in Gaza recorded multiple Israeli air strikes on Tuesday and Wednesday, along with shelling, demolition-related explosions, fire from Israeli naval forces and gunfire in all of the Strip’s governorates, UN Deputy Spokesperson Farhan Haq told the daily briefing in New York. Haq said the UN brought in more fuel, plastic sheeting, winter clothing, medicines and medical supplies on Tuesday but that what it can bring in does not meet needs. Partners report constraints from funding shortfalls and Israeli restrictions, including bans on some partners, limits on importing essential items and restricted access to more than 64 percent of Gaza’s land, he said. In the West Bank, the UN human rights office reported that Israeli forces on Tuesday raided the offices of Palestinian civil society organisations and a medical complex in Ramallah, seizing files and equipment and detaining Palestinians, Haq said.","n":[["64%+","of Gaza’s land to which humanitarian access is restricted","UN Deputy Spokesperson Farhan Haq (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-israeli-forces-raid-azzun-and-tulkarms-shweikeh-suburb.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"21:57","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces raid Azzun and Tulkarm’s Shweikeh suburb, firing tear gas","g":"raid","x":"Troops stopped cars and held passengers in Shweikeh and fired sound and gas grenades in Azzun; a checkpoint at Qalqilya’s southern entrance slowed traffic earlier.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Shweikeh, north of Tulkarm; Azzun, east of Qalqilya","b":"Israeli forces raided the Shweikeh suburb north of Tulkarm on Wednesday evening with several military vehicles, deploying foot patrols, stopping cars and holding their passengers while firing tear gas and rubber-coated metal bullets, local sources said. No injuries or arrests were reported. Forces also raided Azzun, east of Qalqilya, entering from its northern entrance and firing sound and tear-gas grenades in the al-Safha neighbourhood, local sources told WAFA. Earlier in the evening, troops set up a checkpoint at Qalqilya’s southern entrance near the Habla tunnel, stopping cars and checking IDs, local sources said.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-algeria-kuwait-and-arab-parliament-join-condemnation-of.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"23:59","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"Algeria, Kuwait and Arab Parliament join condemnation of Colombia’s Jerusalem embassy","g":"diplomacy","x":"Algeria said the step undermines the two-state solution; Kuwait urged the Security Council to act; the Arab Parliament’s speaker called it null and void.","s":"WAFA (Algerian and Kuwaiti foreign ministries, Arab Parliament)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Algiers, Kuwait and Cairo","b":"Algeria’s Foreign Ministry condemned the Colombian government’s opening of an embassy in Jerusalem, calling it an unacceptable step in repeated, illegitimate attempts to change the city’s legal and historical status that undermines the two-state solution, and urged the Security Council to act. Kuwait’s Foreign Ministry called the opening a flagrant violation of international law and called on the international community and the Security Council to confront attempts to impose a new reality in the occupied Palestinian territory. Arab Parliament Speaker Mohammed al-Yamahi said any unilateral measures to change Jerusalem’s identity or legal status are null and void and called on Colombia to reverse the step immediately, WAFA reported."},{"u":"news/2026-10-14-wafas-daily-round-up-four-killed-in-gaza-dozens.html","d":"2026-10-14","t":"23:59","r":"west-bank","k":"Report","h":"WAFA’s daily round-up: four killed in Gaza, dozens detained in West Bank","g":"raid","x":"The agency’s closing tally lists raids, arrests, settler attacks, demolitions and road closures in the West Bank, and strikes and demolitions across Gaza.","s":"WAFA (daily round-up)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"West Bank and Gaza","b":"WAFA’s end-of-day round-up said four Palestinians, including a woman, were killed in Gaza on Wednesday: two in the drone strike on a car in al-Qarara, one by gunfire in Jabalia camp and a woman in the morning strike on an apartment in al-Rimal. An earlier WAFA report had put the day’s toll at five. It said Israeli forces detained dozens of people across the West Bank, including children, and listed raids in Nablus, Qalqilya, Hebron, Jerusalem, Tulkarm, Tubas and Salfit; settler attacks in Mukhmas, Ya’bad, Beit Dajan, Burin, Turmus Ayya, Yarza, Qarawat Bani Hassan and Beit Furik; and demolitions in Masafer Yatta and Haris. The round-up also recorded the continued closure of Tuqu’s seven entrances, the new gate at Wad Da’uq and the checkpoint at Qalqilya’s southern entrance.","n":[["4","Palestinians killed in Gaza on 14 Oct, per the round-up","WAFA"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-haaretz-editorial-barring-displaced-palestinians-from-going-home.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"00:23","r":"west-bank","k":"Statement","h":"Haaretz editorial: barring displaced Palestinians from going home would be population transfer","g":"humanitarian","x":"The Israeli daily’s editors say a mass displacement is taking place in the West Bank in practice, with little public attention.","s":"Haaretz (editorial)","sp":["Haaretz"],"p":"Northern occupied West Bank refugee camps","b":"The Israeli daily Haaretz said in an editorial published just after midnight that ‘an ongoing mass displacement of Palestinians from their homes is taking place in practice in the West Bank’, with little public attention. ‘Not giving them the opportunity to return would constitute population transfer, which is defined as a war crime,’ the editors wrote, warning that Israel could end up ‘in the dock’. The editorial ran hours after Israel’s High Court asked the state for a timetable for the return of more than 33,000 people displaced from the Jenin, Tulkarm and Nur Shams camps, and after Defense Minister Israel Katz called the army’s presence there ‘permanent’.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-four-year-old-boy-dies-of-wounds-from-israeli-strike.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"07:21","r":"gaza","k":"Update","h":"Four-year-old boy dies of wounds from Israeli strike on al-Zawaida three weeks ago","g":"killed-injured","x":"Two men were shot and wounded in Khan Younis; the Israeli military said its strikes had killed five militants in recent days.","s":"WAFA / Gaza medical sources; The Times of Israel","sp":["WAFA","Gaza medical sources","The Times of Israel"],"p":"al-Zawaida, central Gaza Strip; al-Amal and Hamad City, Khan Younis","b":"A four-year-old boy died on Tuesday of wounds he suffered in an Israeli strike on al-Zawaida, in the central Gaza Strip, about three weeks earlier, medical sources told WAFA. Two men were shot and wounded by Israeli forces in the al-Amal neighbourhood and near Hamad City, north of Khan Younis, WAFA reported, and Quds News Network said a third man was wounded near the al-Isra tower in the city. The Israeli military said on Tuesday morning that five militants had been killed in its strikes in recent days, three Islamic Jihad members in Khan Younis on Sunday and two Hamas members in southern Gaza, and accused them of planning attacks ‘in systematic violation of the ceasefire’, The Times of Israel reported."},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-israeli-forces-detain-28-in-villages-around-nablus.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"08:12","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces detain 28 in villages around Nablus, turning a home into an interrogation centre","g":"detention","x":"Fifteen were taken from Tell and 12 from Asira al-Shamaliya; the Israeli army said separate raids caught four Islamic Jihad members.","s":"WAFA (security sources); Quds News Network; The Times of Israel","sp":["WAFA","Quds News Network","The Times of Israel"],"p":"Tell and Asira al-Shamaliya, Nablus governorate","b":"Israeli forces detained 28 Palestinians in pre-dawn raids on villages southwest and north of Nablus on Tuesday, security sources told WAFA: 15 in Tell, 12 in Asira al-Shamaliya, where troops raided several homes and turned one into a field interrogation centre, and one in a village south of the city. Troops also detained a young man in Tubas after raiding his family’s home and searched a house in Tammun, and detained a man in Hebron and another in al-Rashayida, east of Bethlehem, WAFA reported. The Israeli military said troops had arrested four Islamic Jihad members planning an attack in raids on Qabatiya and Arraba this week, and an arms dealer in Beit Rima, The Times of Israel reported.","n":[["28","Palestinians detained in villages around Nablus","Security sources (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-settlers-posing-as-soldiers-beat-a-talfit-family.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"08:35","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers posing as soldiers beat a Talfit family in their home overnight","g":"settlers","x":"The father said masked men attacked him, his wife and their two daughters, aged 14 and 18, while the army was operating in the village.","s":"WAFA (local sources); The Times of Israel; Haaretz","sp":["WAFA","The Times of Israel","Haaretz"],"p":"Talfit, south of Nablus","b":"Settlers dressed as Israeli soldiers knocked on the door of a family home in Talfit, south of Nablus, before dawn on Tuesday and beat the family, injuring several of them, local sources told WAFA. The father told The Times of Israel that about five masked settlers attacked the household at around 3 a.m. while Israeli troops were operating in the village, beating him, his wife and their daughters aged 14 and 18. His wife needed 30 stitches to a head wound, one daughter had a broken leg and the other chest injuries, and all four were taken to hospital, he said. The Israeli military had not commented, the paper said. A neighbour told Haaretz that the settlers identified themselves as soldiers to get into the house, and a military source confirmed the army was operating in the area at the time, the paper reported.","q":"They grabbed me and tried to choke me.","w":"The father of the family, Talfit resident, to The Times of Israel","n":[["4","family members hospitalised after the attack, per the father","The Times of Israel"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-israeli-bulldozers-finish-demolishing-a-home-for-16.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"08:40","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"Israeli bulldozers finish demolishing a home for 16 in al-Tur","g":"demolition","x":"The family had been forced to start knocking down the 200-square-metre house themselves two days earlier, local sources said.","s":"WAFA (local sources)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Sheikh Anbar neighbourhood, al-Tur, occupied East Jerusalem","b":"A large Israeli force with demolition machinery completed the demolition of a family home in the Sheikh Anbar neighbourhood of al-Tur, on the Mount of Olives in occupied East Jerusalem, on Tuesday morning, after the family had begun demolishing it themselves, local sources told WAFA. Two days earlier Israeli authorities forced the owner to demolish the 200-square-metre house, home to 16 people for 20 years, WAFA said.","n":[["16","people who lived in the demolished al-Tur home","WAFA (local sources)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-dozens-of-settlers-attack-turmus-ayya-to-keep.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"09:20","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Dozens of settlers attack Turmus Ayya to keep residents from groves behind the wall","g":"olive-harvest","x":"It was the second day running that Turmus Ayya farmers were kept from their olives.","s":"WAFA (local sources)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Turmus Ayya, north of Ramallah","b":"Dozens of settlers attacked the town of Turmus Ayya, north of Ramallah, on Tuesday morning to stop residents from reaching their land behind the separation wall to pick olives, local sources told WAFA. The Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission later listed Turmus Ayya among the day’s attacks, saying settlers stole olives and harvesting equipment, broke trees and drove owners off their land. WAFA cited the Commission as recording 647 settler attacks across the West Bank in September.","n":[["647","settler attacks in the West Bank in September 2026","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-settlers-drive-beit-imrin-olive-pickers-off-their.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"09:55","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers drive Beit Imrin olive pickers off their land at gunpoint, inside Area A","g":"olive-harvest","x":"The groves lie in Area A, which is under Palestinian Authority control, local sources noted.","s":"WAFA (local sources)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"al-Naqb area, Beit Imrin, north of Nablus","b":"Settlers attacked olive pickers in the al-Naqb area of Beit Imrin, north of Nablus, on Tuesday and forced them off their land at gunpoint, local sources told WAFA. The sources noted that the land lies in Area A, which is under Palestinian Authority control. The sources said settlers repeatedly attack farmers in al-Naqb and keep them from reaching their land."},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-israeli-court-gives-15-year-old-from-issawiya-six-months.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"10:34","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"Israeli court gives 15-year-old from Issawiya six months in prison","g":"prisoners","x":"The boy had been held in October 2025 and released in December under open-ended house arrest, local sources said.","s":"WAFA (local sources)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Issawiya, occupied East Jerusalem","b":"An Israeli court sentenced a 15-year-old boy from Issawiya, in occupied East Jerusalem, to six months in prison, local sources told WAFA. The boy was detained on 15 October 2025 and released on 30 December under open-ended house arrest before the sentence was handed down, the sources said. Israeli forces also raided the nearby neighbourhood of al-Tur with military vehicles on Tuesday, WAFA reported.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-israeli-forces-surround-ramallahs-main-hospital-and-raid.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"10:35","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces surround Ramallah’s main hospital and raid rights groups; teen shot, man seriously hurt","g":"raid","x":"Two journalists were injured and dozens treated for tear gas during the hours-long raid, the Health Ministry and local sources said.","s":"WAFA / Palestinian Health Ministry; Quds News Network","sp":["WAFA","Palestinian Health Ministry","Quds News Network"],"p":"Ramallah: Palestine Medical Complex and al-Tashri'i roundabout","b":"Israeli forces entered the courtyard of the Palestine Medical Complex in Ramallah on Tuesday morning, posted snipers at its gates and stopped people from entering or leaving, local sources told WAFA. Troops also moved through al-Bireh, al-Tira and Surda. At the al-Tashri’i roundabout in central Ramallah, soldiers fired live and rubber-coated bullets and tear gas and targeted journalists, local sources said: a Palestine TV reporter was hit in the hand by a stun grenade and a Sky News Arabia reporter in the foot by fragments of a gas canister fired directly at him. Tear gas was also fired into the Ramallah boys’ school, WAFA said. A young man was seriously wounded by live fire and a 14-year-old boy was shot in the upper thigh, and dozens were treated for tear gas inhalation, the Palestinian Health Ministry said. Troops raided the offices of Addameer, the prisoners’ rights group Israel closed in 2022, and seized equipment and files, WAFA reported, and also raided the Union of Palestinian Women’s Committees, according to Quds News Network. Two men were detained after being beaten in the street, WAFA said.","n":[["2","people shot with live fire, one seriously, the other a 14-year-old","Palestinian Health Ministry (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-settlers-smash-a-masafer-yatta-school-vehicle-and.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"11:49","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers smash a Masafer Yatta school vehicle and steal a family’s olives; troops raze terraces near Hebron","g":"settlers","x":"The vehicle carries pupils to the school in al-Fakheit, local sources said.","s":"WAFA (local sources)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Khirbet Qawawis and Khallet al-Hummus; Khirbet Suba, west of Hebron","b":"Settlers damaged crops and fences in the Qawawis area of Masafer Yatta, south of Hebron, attacked a family picking olives and stole the harvest, local sources told WAFA. They also smashed the windows of a vehicle that takes pupils to the al-Fakheit school, and blocked the road to a family’s home in Khallet al-Hummus, south of Yatta, the sources said. In Khirbet Suba, west of Hebron, Israeli forces demolished a farm room and a water tank, wrecked stone terraces across a wide area and uprooted olive, almond and other fruit trees, WAFA reported.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-settlers-beat-two-women-picking-olives-in-beit.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"12:02","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers beat two women picking olives in Beit Lid; one pulls a gun on the family","g":"olive-harvest","x":"An elderly woman was hit on the head; nearby, settlers forced farmers and foreign volunteers out of the Ramin plain at gunpoint, witnesses said.","s":"WAFA / Palestine Red Crescent Society","sp":["WAFA","Palestine Red Crescent Society"],"p":"Beit Lid and Ramin plain, east of Tulkarm","b":"Two women were injured when settlers beat them while they were picking olives in Beit Lid, east of Tulkarm, on Tuesday, the Palestine Red Crescent Society said. Its crews took them to hospital with injuries to the abdomen and head, and their condition was described as moderate. Witnesses told WAFA that armed settlers carrying sticks attacked the pickers on the edge of the town, that one of the women hit was elderly, and that a settler pointed his gun at relatives trying to get her away. In Wadi al-Jami’ and the Ramin plain, also east of Tulkarm, settlers forced farmers off their land at gunpoint, stopping them from picking olives, and threatened them not to return, witnesses told WAFA. Foreign volunteers had been helping the farmers with the harvest, the witnesses said.","n":[["2","women injured by settlers while picking olives in Beit Lid","Palestine Red Crescent Society (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-israeli-forces-hold-four-journalists-covering-settler-attacks.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"12:10","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces hold four journalists covering settler attacks on olive pickers in Idhna","g":"olive-harvest","x":"Troops declared the area a closed military zone and barred farmers and foreign volunteers from the harvest, local sources said.","s":"WAFA (local sources)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"al-Nabi Salih area, west of Idhna, Hebron governorate","b":"Israeli forces held four Palestinian journalists in Idhna, west of Hebron, on Tuesday while they were covering settler attacks on olive pickers, and stopped them from reporting on the grounds that the area was a closed military zone, local sources told WAFA. Troops also stopped residents and foreign activists from picking olives in the al-Nabi Salih area west of the town, the sources said.","n":[["4","journalists held while covering the olive harvest","WAFA (local sources)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-133-israelis-enter-al-aqsa-under-police-protection-jerusalem.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"12:11","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"133 Israelis enter Al-Aqsa under police protection, Jerusalem Governorate says","g":"settlers","x":"More than 888 entered the previous week, according to Quds News Network.","s":"WAFA / Jerusalem Governorate; Quds News Network","sp":["WAFA","Jerusalem Governorate","Quds News Network"],"p":"Al-Aqsa Mosque compound, Old City of occupied East Jerusalem","b":"A total of 133 Israelis, described by the Jerusalem Governorate as settlers, entered the Al-Aqsa Mosque compound through the Mughrabi Gate on Tuesday morning under the protection of Israeli forces and performed Talmudic rituals and provocative tours, the governorate said, while Israeli authorities restricted Muslim worshippers. The groups entered in waves and left through the Chain Gate, and more than 888 had entered the compound the previous week, Quds News Network reported.","n":[["133","Israelis who entered the Al-Aqsa compound on the morning of 13 Oct","Jerusalem Governorate (via WAFA)"],["888+","Israelis who entered the compound the previous week","Quds News Network"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-israeli-bulldozers-demolish-homes-of-a-father-and.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"12:18","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"Israeli bulldozers demolish homes of a father and son in Silwan","g":"demolition","x":"The son’s 100-square-metre home housed his wife and four children; demolition orders were served the day before.","s":"WAFA / Jerusalem Governorate","sp":["WAFA","Jerusalem Governorate"],"p":"Wadi Qaddum, Silwan, occupied East Jerusalem","b":"Israeli bulldozers escorted by troops entered the Wadi Qaddum neighbourhood of Silwan, south of Al-Aqsa, on Tuesday and demolished two homes belonging to a man and his father, the Jerusalem Governorate said. The orders had been served on Monday and the son had started emptying both homes; his 100-square-metre house, built in 2002, was home to his wife and four children, and his parents’ house measured 60 square metres, the governorate said on Monday.","n":[["2","homes demolished in Silwan","Jerusalem Governorate (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-israeli-drone-strike-on-a-car-in-gaza.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"12:27","r":"gaza","k":"Update","h":"Israeli drone strike on a car in Gaza City’s al-Tuffah kills one, medical sources say","g":"killed-injured","x":"Heavy fire was reported south of Khan Younis and shelling in eastern Gaza City, Deir al-Balah and Beit Lahiya.","s":"WAFA / Gaza medical sources; Quds News Network","sp":["WAFA","Gaza medical sources","Quds News Network"],"p":"al-Mushahara Street, al-Tuffah, northeast Gaza City","b":"One Palestinian was killed and several others injured when an Israeli drone struck a civilian car on al-Mushahara Street in al-Tuffah, northeast of Gaza City, on Tuesday, medical sources told WAFA. Israeli vehicles opened heavy fire south of Khan Younis and an explosion was heard in Jabalia, WAFA reported. Quds News Network said an air strike also hit the entrance of a shop near the municipal park in central Gaza City without causing injuries, that Israeli artillery shelled eastern Deir al-Balah, eastern Gaza City and western Beit Lahiya, and that Israeli forces blew up buildings east of Jabalia camp.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-displacement-is-like-you-do-not-have-a.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"12:51","r":"gaza","k":"Story","h":"‘Displacement is like you do not have a homeland’: Gazans tell the Red Cross about three years of war","g":"humanitarian","x":"A father who stayed behind follows his son’s treatment abroad from a distance; a Red Crescent driver remembers the patients he took to the border.","s":"ICRC (feature)","sp":["ICRC"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"Three years after the escalation of the war, people in Gaza described to the International Committee of the Red Cross what they have lived through, in a feature published on Tuesday. A 34-year-old woman said she left home with only her ID card and a few clothes, expecting to return in two days; more than a year later she still lives in a tent. A 74-year-old man recalled the meals his late wife cooked to gather their children and grandchildren. ‘That was home,’ he said. A 38-year-old father said goodbye in 2025 to his wife and 11-year-old son, who was medically evacuated for treatment abroad, and has followed the boy’s care from Gaza ever since. A Palestine Red Crescent ambulance team leader who takes patients to the border crossing for medical evacuation told the ICRC that many have waited months for treatment by the time his crews reach them. Waiting lists for specialist care or evacuation remain in the thousands, the ICRC said.","q":"Displacement is like you do not have a homeland. It is like you have no land. You have no family. You have no safety.","w":"A 74-year-old displaced man in Gaza, to the ICRC","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-displaced-gaza-families-rebuild-tents-with-steel-pulled.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"12:59","r":"gaza","k":"Story","h":"Displaced Gaza families rebuild tents with steel pulled from rubble before winter","g":"humanitarian","x":"In al-Mawasi, families are paying hundreds of shekels for salvaged steel frames and tarpaulins as the rainy season nears, WAFA reports.","s":"WAFA (feature)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"al-Mawasi, west of Khan Younis, southern Gaza Strip","b":"Displaced families in al-Mawasi, the coastal strip west of Khan Younis, are rebuilding their tents on frames of steel bars pulled from the rubble of destroyed homes, hoping the shelters will hold against wind and rain this winter, WAFA reported. Families told the agency that the high price of timber had pushed them toward salvaged steel. A father who supports a family of seven told WAFA he borrowed money to rebuild a 4-by-6-metre tent on a steel frame for about 800 shekels, with steel selling at four shekels a metre. Covering it takes four tarpaulins at about 200 shekels each, he said. Another man said his new tent cost 2,000 shekels; others said they could afford only tarpaulins and nylon sheeting, or were still waiting for tarpaulins aid groups had promised. UN OCHA reported on 2 October that about 279,000 people in Gaza were sheltering in 400 displacement sites at risk of flooding.","n":[["800","shekels to rebuild a 4 x 6 m tent on a salvaged-steel frame (one family’s cost)","WAFA"],["200","shekels per tarpaulin; four are needed to cover one tent","WAFA"],["279,000","people sheltering in 400 flood-prone displacement sites in Gaza","UN OCHA"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-gaza-death-toll-since-october-2023-reaches-74389.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"13:15","r":"gaza","k":"Figures","h":"Gaza death toll since October 2023 reaches 74,389, medical sources say","g":"killed-injured","x":"Seven dead, three of them from earlier wounds, reached hospitals in 24 hours; 1,498 have been killed since the ceasefire.","s":"WAFA / Gaza medical sources","sp":["WAFA","Gaza medical sources"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"The number of Palestinians killed in Gaza since 7 October 2023 has risen to 74,389, with 175,423 injured, medical sources told WAFA on Tuesday. Hospitals received seven dead in the previous 24 hours, three of whom died of earlier wounds and one whose body was recovered, and seven injured, the sources said. Since the ceasefire of 11 October 2025, 1,498 people have been killed and 5,306 injured, and 838 bodies have been recovered, they said.","n":[["74,389","Palestinians killed in Gaza since 7 Oct 2023 (175,423 injured)","Gaza medical sources (via WAFA)"],["1,498","killed since the 11 Oct 2025 ceasefire (5,306 injured)","Gaza medical sources (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-israeli-police-arrest-suspect-over-attack-on-a.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"13:20","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli police arrest suspect over attack on a Palestinian driver and theft of a village car in Yabrud","g":"settlers","x":"Police said they found suspected stolen military gear in his car; WAFA said olives and tools were taken along with the village council’s vehicle.","s":"The Times of Israel / Israeli police","sp":["The Times of Israel","Israeli police"],"p":"Yabrud, northeast of Ramallah","b":"Israeli police arrested a man in his 20s from the northern West Bank on suspicion of taking part in an attack on a Palestinian in Yabrud on Sunday, in which an Israeli vehicle rammed his car and masked assailants robbed it and stoned him, The Times of Israel reported. Police stopped the suspect on Route 60 near the Ofra settlement and said they found suspected stolen military equipment, an air gun, a dagger, pepper spray, a drone and slingshots in his car. WAFA had reported that the car belonged to the Yabrud village council and was being used by residents during the olive harvest, and that settlers also took bags of olives and farm tools from it, the paper said."},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-cabinet-cites-719-attacks-in-one-week-and.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"13:22","r":"west-bank","k":"Figures","h":"Cabinet cites 719 attacks in one week and 38,000 women killed in three years of war","g":"settlers","x":"The weekly session also backed a statement by 100 aid groups calling the Gaza ceasefire ‘ink on paper’ and set winter time from 24 October.","s":"WAFA / Palestinian cabinet","sp":["WAFA","Palestinian cabinet"],"p":"Ramallah","b":"Prime Minister Mohammad Mustafa told the weekly cabinet session on Tuesday that 719 attacks on Palestinians and their property were documented between 4 and 10 October, including burned vehicles, attacks on olive pickers, new outposts and the demolition of 27 structures, citing Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission data, WAFA reported. The Ministry of Women’s Affairs told the cabinet its records show 38,000 women killed in three years of war in Gaza, one in 21 women killed or disabled, and 22,000 women who lost their husbands, according to WAFA. The cabinet welcomed a statement by 100 international aid and rights groups that called the Gaza ceasefire ‘ink on paper’ and said about 1.4 million people face acute hunger. The cabinet also approved larger purchases of medicines for diabetes, osteoporosis and other conditions, and set the switch to winter time for dawn on Saturday 24 October, WAFA said.","n":[["719","attacks on Palestinians and their property, 4-10 Oct; 27 structures demolished","Palestinian cabinet / Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"],["38,000","women killed in Gaza in three years of war","Palestinian Ministry of Women's Affairs (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-grand-mufti-rules-that-buying-olives-or-oil.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"13:49","r":"jerusalem","k":"Statement","h":"Grand Mufti rules that buying olives or oil stolen by settlers is forbidden","g":"olive-harvest","x":"Sheikh Mohammad Hussein said the purchase would empower those taking Palestinian land.","s":"WAFA / Grand Mufti of Jerusalem and Palestine","sp":["WAFA","Grand Mufti of Jerusalem and Palestine"],"p":"Jerusalem","b":"The Grand Mufti of Jerusalem and Palestine, Sheikh Mohammad Hussein, issued a fatwa on Tuesday forbidding Palestinians to buy olives or olive oil taken from Palestinian trees and sold by settlers, WAFA reported. He said buying stolen goods is forbidden because the seller does not own them, and that such purchases encourage the seizure of Palestinian land and the displacement of its owners. He called on official bodies and the public to refuse to buy or sell such produce.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-arab-league-calls-for-international-protection-for-palestinians.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"16:38","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"Arab League calls for international protection for Palestinians and condemns Colombia’s embassy plan","g":"diplomacy","x":"Meeting at Palestine’s request, the council urged the Security Council to act under Chapter VII and called for a ‘Uniting for Peace’ session of the General Assembly.","s":"WAFA / Arab League","sp":["WAFA","Arab League"],"p":"Arab League headquarters, Cairo","b":"The Arab League Council, meeting in an extraordinary session of permanent representatives in Cairo at Palestine’s request, called on the parties to the Fourth Geneva Convention to intervene immediately to protect Palestinians from Israeli attacks and settler violence, WAFA reported. Tunisia chaired the session. The council asked the UN Security Council to adopt a resolution under Chapter VII obliging Israel to comply with its resolutions, called for an emergency special session of the General Assembly under ‘Uniting for Peace’, and condemned settler attacks on farmers during the olive harvest, WAFA said. It strongly condemned Colombia’s intention to open an embassy in Jerusalem, called it a grave violation of international law, and urged Arab and Islamic states to summon Colombia’s ambassadors to protest, according to WAFA. The embassy opened the next day.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-troops-fire-tear-gas-at-salfit-olive-pickers.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"17:06","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Troops fire tear gas at Salfit olive pickers and detain a farmer; settlers steal olives in Yasuf and Atara","g":"olive-harvest","x":"In Yarza, east of Tubas, residents stopped settlers from driving off a flock of sheep.","s":"Quds News Network; WAFA (local sources)","sp":["Quds News Network","WAFA"],"p":"Salfit, Yasuf and al-Lubban al-Sharqiya; Atara; Yarza","b":"Israeli forces fired tear gas at farmers picking olives north of Salfit on Tuesday, forced them off their land and detained a farmer while he was in his grove, local sources told WAFA. Quds News Network said the farmers had arrived with prior coordination and that troops also fired shots at them. In Yasuf, east of Salfit, settlers stole olives in the al-Nasba area, WAFA reported, and Quds News Network said settlers also bulldozed land in the valley and took harvesting equipment. The village council of al-Lubban al-Sharqiya warned residents that settlers were picking olives on the edge of Wadi al-Sha’ir, the network said. Settlers also stole olives from Palestinian land in Atara, northwest of Ramallah, local sources told WAFA. In the Yarza community east of Tubas, settlers tried to steal a resident’s flock but residents confronted them and recovered the animals, local sources said.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-gaza-olive-press-damaged-in-the-war-restarts.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"17:46","r":"gaza","k":"Story","h":"Gaza olive press damaged in the war restarts one of its seven lines","g":"olive-harvest","x":"WAFA footage from west of al-Zawaida shows farmers bringing in this season’s olives while much farmland stays out of reach.","s":"WAFA (video report)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Olive press west of al-Zawaida, central Gaza Strip","b":"Farmers in central Gaza are carrying this season’s olives to a press west of al-Zawaida that its owner has partly brought back into service, according to a WAFA video report. He has restarted one of the press’s seven production lines; WAFA says the press was destroyed by Israeli forces. The report says farmland and olive presses across the Strip were heavily damaged, and that farmers still cannot reach land under Israeli military control. A joint statement by the Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics and the Environment Quality Authority on 14 October, citing FAO and UNOSAT assessments, said only 4,480 dunums of Gaza’s farmland, about 3 percent of the total, were accessible and undamaged as of 24 June 2026.","n":[["1 of 7","production lines restarted at the damaged press","WAFA"],["3%","share of Gaza’s farmland accessible and undamaged (4,480 dunums), per FAO/UNOSAT","PCBS and Environment Quality Authority"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-us-state-department-claims-real-progress-toward-a.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"18:00","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"US State Department claims ‘real progress’ toward a demilitarised Gaza a year after Sharm el-Sheikh","g":"diplomacy","x":"It said rebuilding will be ‘an arduous, multi-year process’; the claims were not independently verified.","s":"The Times of Israel / US State Department","sp":["The Times of Israel","US State Department"],"p":"Washington","b":"The US State Department said on the anniversary of the Sharm el-Sheikh summit that ‘real progress’ is being made toward a demilitarised Gaza, The Times of Israel reported. It credited the Trump administration’s plan with producing a lasting ceasefire, the return of all hostages, the creation of the Board of Peace and a Palestinian technocratic administration, and a fully costed recovery plan, and said Hamas theft of aid had ‘dwindled’. ‘Rebuilding Gaza will be an arduous, multi-year process,’ the statement said. The paper noted that the claims were the department’s own. Gaza health officials have counted 1,498 people killed since the ceasefire, according to medical sources quoted by WAFA the same day."},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-a-year-after-the-prisoner-exchange-about-80.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"18:06","r":"prisoners","k":"Statement","h":"A year after the prisoner exchange, about 80 freed prisoners have been re-arrested or summoned","g":"prisoners","x":"The 13 October 2025 exchange freed 1,968 Palestinians, 250 of them serving life sentences, the Prisoners’ Society said.","s":"Quds News Network / Palestinian Prisoners' Society","sp":["Quds News Network","Palestinian Prisoners' Society"],"p":"West Bank and Israeli prisons","b":"Israeli authorities have re-arrested, summoned or interrogated about 80 Palestinians freed in previous prisoner exchanges, the Palestinian Prisoners’ Society said on Tuesday, the first anniversary of the 13 October 2025 exchange, according to Quds News Network. That exchange freed 1,968 Palestinian prisoners, 250 of them serving life sentences, and the three exchanges since November 2023 freed 3,985 in all, the society said. It said families of prisoners deported abroad on release are still unable to see them. Separately, the Prisoners’ Media Office said a detained Gaza doctor was shackled and beaten by guards in Ganot prison on 7 October during a visit by National Security Minister Itamar Ben-Gvir, the network reported.","n":[["~80","freed prisoners re-arrested, summoned or interrogated","Palestinian Prisoners' Society (via Quds News Network)"],["1,968","prisoners freed in the 13 Oct 2025 exchange, 250 of them serving life","Palestinian Prisoners' Society (via Quds News Network)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-netanyahu-visiting-near-tulkarm-says-israel-will-keep.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"19:36","r":"west-bank","k":"Statement","h":"Netanyahu, visiting near Tulkarm, says Israel will keep control of the area: ‘Our country, our control’","g":"raid","x":"He warned Palestinian Authority forces against turning their weapons on Israeli troops before the election.","s":"The Times of Israel","sp":["The Times of Israel"],"p":"Area near Tulkarm, on the edge of the occupied West Bank","b":"Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, visiting the area near Tulkarm with Defense Minister Israel Katz and senior security officials, said the army will keep control of the Tulkarm area, where it has carried out extensive operations, The Times of Israel reported. Netanyahu said Israel was ready for possible attacks in the West Bank, including by Palestinian Authority security forces turning their guns on Israeli troops, and vowed a response of ‘a level of force you have never imagined’. ‘There will be no Arab army west of the Jordan River,’ he said, according to the paper. The Times of Israel noted that attacks on Israelis by members of the PA forces are rare.","q":"Our country, our control.","w":"Benjamin Netanyahu, Prime Minister of Israel","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-minister-says-about-74000-gaza-children-under-five.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"20:00","r":"gaza","k":"Statement","h":"Minister says about 74,000 Gaza children under five are malnourished","g":"humanitarian","x":"Social Development Minister Samah Hamad also told an Arab League meeting that the number displaced in the West Bank is nearing 50,000.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"About 74,000 children under the age of five in Gaza are suffering from malnutrition, Palestinian Social Development Minister Samah Hamad said in a recorded address to the executive bureau of the Arab Council of Social Affairs Ministers, meeting at Arab League headquarters in Cairo on Tuesday. Hamad said families have lived in tents for three years and that the food aid entering Gaza does not meet their needs. In the West Bank, she said, the number of people displaced by Israeli raids and attacks is approaching 50,000. She said the government keeps providing basic services despite a financial crisis and the withholding of about $6.5 billion in Palestinian funds, and that the ministry’s social registry covers about 350,000 families. The bureau approved a ministry programme of cash aid for displaced people with disabilities in Tulkarm and Jenin, to be put to the ministerial council, Palestine’s delegate said.","n":[["74,000","children under five in Gaza suffering from malnutrition","Palestinian Ministry of Social Development (Samah Hamad)"],["50,000","people displaced in the West Bank (approaching)","Palestinian Ministry of Social Development (Samah Hamad)"],["$6.5 billion","Palestinian funds withheld (approx.)","Palestinian Ministry of Social Development (Samah Hamad)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-israeli-military-vehicle-runs-over-three-palestinians-in.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"21:02","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli military vehicle runs over three Palestinians in al-Yamun, Red Crescent says","g":"killed-injured","x":"One of the three suffered fractures to his feet during an evening raid on the town west of Jenin, the Palestine Red Crescent Society said.","s":"WAFA / Palestine Red Crescent Society","sp":["WAFA","Palestine Red Crescent Society"],"p":"al-Yamun, west of Jenin","b":"Three Palestinians were injured when an Israeli military vehicle ran them over during a raid on the town of al-Yamun, west of Jenin, on Tuesday evening, the Palestine Red Crescent Society said. They were taken to the government hospital in Jenin; one had fractures to his feet. Earlier that day Israeli forces raided al-Yamun, Silat al-Harithiya, Kafr Dan and Burqin, searched homes and held young men and former prisoners for field interrogation inside a house in al-Yamun, WAFA reported.","n":[["3","Palestinians injured when a military vehicle ran them over","Palestine Red Crescent Society (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-australia-canada-france-and-britain-urge-abbas-to.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"22:32","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"Australia, Canada, France and Britain urge Abbas to reverse the election delay","g":"diplomacy","x":"The four foreign ministers said a whole generation of Palestinians has reached adulthood without a vote, and asked Israel not to obstruct elections.","s":"Haaretz; joint statement (Australian Foreign Minister's office, via ReliefWeb)","sp":["Haaretz","joint statement"],"p":"Ramallah","b":"The foreign ministers of Australia, Canada, France and the United Kingdom said they ‘deeply regret’ President Mahmoud Abbas’s decision to postpone legislative and presidential elections to September 2027, and called on the Palestinian Authority to reconsider and hold them ‘without further delay’, in a joint statement reported by Haaretz. They said nearly two decades have passed since the last legislative elections and that ‘an entire generation of Palestinians has reached adulthood’ without choosing its representatives. The ministers called on Israel to refrain from actions that could impede the elections, including the arrangements needed for all Palestinians to take part, and repeated that Hamas can have no role in governing Palestine."},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-palestinian-officials-win-court-order-to-remove-settler.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"23:04","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Palestinian officials win court order to remove settler fence in northern Jordan Valley","g":"settlers","x":"Settlers had fenced land of the Nabe’ Ghazal community in al-Farisiya; Israeli forces took the fence down after the ruling, a Tubas official said.","s":"WAFA / Tubas Governorate","sp":["WAFA","Tubas Governorate"],"p":"al-Farisiya, northern Jordan Valley","b":"The Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission and Tubas Governorate obtained a court ruling on Tuesday ordering the removal of a fence that settlers had put up on land belonging to the Nabe’ Ghazal community in al-Farisiya, in the northern Jordan Valley, WAFA reported. Mutaz Basharat, who handles settlement affairs for Tubas Governorate, said Israeli forces removed the fence after the ruling. The report did not name the court."},{"u":"news/2026-10-13-wafas-daily-round-up-one-killed-in-gaza-commission.html","d":"2026-10-13","t":"23:53","r":"west-bank","k":"Report","h":"WAFA’s daily round-up: one killed in Gaza; Commission counts 25 attacks on farmers in seven governorates","g":"raid","x":"The closing tallies cover the Ramallah raid, settler attacks from Talfit to Masafer Yatta, and demolitions in Jerusalem and Jenin.","s":"WAFA (daily round-up); Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission","sp":["WAFA","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission"],"p":"West Bank and Gaza","b":"WAFA’s end-of-day round-up said one Palestinian was killed in the strike on a car in Gaza City’s al-Tuffah on Tuesday and listed the injuries in the Ramallah raid, the arrests around Nablus, Hebron and Bethlehem, and an incursion into Tuqu’, southeast of Bethlehem. It also recorded the demolition of an uninhabited house in al-Jalama, north of Jenin, of greenhouses near the bypass roundabout, and the installation of an iron gate at the main entrance to Arraba, south of Jenin. The Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission said it recorded 25 attacks on farmers and land by settlers and Israeli forces between 6 a.m. and 6 p.m. in seven governorates: six each in Nablus and Hebron, five in Ramallah and al-Bireh, three each in Salfit and Tulkarm, and one each in Jerusalem and Jenin. They included attacks in Yatma, Duma and Aqraba near Nablus, olive theft by settlers from Susya in Yatta, and farmers barred from their land in Beit Anan, northwest of Jerusalem.","n":[["25","attacks on farmers and land in seven governorates, 06:00-18:00 on 13 Oct","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israeli-drone-strike-kills-two-in-rafahs-al-mawasi.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"07:52","r":"gaza","k":"Update","h":"Israeli drone strike kills two in Rafah’s al-Mawasi area, hospital sources say","g":"killed-injured","x":"The Israeli military said it had targeted militants; a man wounded in northern Gaza a day earlier also died, medical sources told WAFA.","s":"WAFA / Nasser Medical Complex; The Times of Israel","sp":["WAFA","Nasser Medical Complex","The Times of Israel"],"p":"al-Mawasi area of Rafah, southern Gaza Strip","b":"Two Palestinians were killed and several others wounded on Monday morning when an Israeli drone struck a group of people in the al-Mawasi area of Rafah, in the southern Gaza Strip, sources at Nasser Medical Complex told WAFA. The Israeli military said it had targeted ‘terror operatives’ and that the results of the strike were under review, The Times of Israel reported, adding that Palestinian media described the strike as hitting near a mosque in the Rafah area and wounding three people. Later in the morning a medical source told WAFA that a man wounded by an Israeli drone strike in northern Gaza on Sunday had died of his injuries, and that another man was shot and wounded by Israeli forces near the Abu Hamid roundabout, east of Khan Younis. Gaza health officials say at least 1,400 Palestinians have been killed by Israeli fire since the ceasefire took effect in October 2025, while Israel says four of its soldiers have been killed in Gaza in the same period, according to a Reuters report carried by Haaretz.","n":[["2","Palestinians killed in the drone strike in Rafah’s al-Mawasi area","Nasser Medical Complex sources (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-troops-turn-turmus-ayya-farmers-away-from-their.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"08:45","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Troops turn Turmus Ayya farmers away from their olive groves; settlers injure a picker","g":"olive-harvest","x":"Israeli forces ordered farmers out of the al-Shuna area in the morning, and a young man was later hurt by settlers while harvesting, local sources said.","s":"WAFA (local sources)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Turmus Ayya, north of Ramallah","b":"Israeli forces stopped farmers from entering their land in the al-Shuna area of Turmus Ayya, north of Ramallah, on Monday morning and forced those already there to leave before they could pick their olives, local sources told WAFA. Later in the morning a young man was wounded and bruised when settlers attacked him while he was harvesting olives in the town, and he was taken to hospital, local sources said. WAFA cited the Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission as saying it had recorded 19 settler attacks on olive pickers and farmers two days earlier, and that attacks by the army and settlers damaged 21,508 trees in August 2026, 19,375 of them olive trees.","n":[["21,508","trees damaged by Israeli army and settler attacks in August 2026, 19,375 of them olive trees","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israelis-perform-prayers-in-front-of-the-dome.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"08:52","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"Israelis perform prayers in front of the Dome of the Rock under police guard, Awqaf says","g":"settlers","x":"Jerusalem’s Islamic Endowments department said a uniformed Israeli soldier took part in the morning rituals at Al-Aqsa.","s":"WAFA (local sources); Quds News Network / Islamic Endowments department","sp":["WAFA","Quds News Network","Islamic Endowments department"],"p":"Al-Aqsa Mosque compound, Old City of occupied East Jerusalem","b":"Dozens of Israelis, described by WAFA as settlers, entered the Al-Aqsa Mosque compound through the Mughrabi Gate on Monday morning under the protection of Israeli forces and performed Talmudic prayers and rituals in front of the Dome of the Rock, local sources told WAFA. The Islamic Endowments (Awqaf) department in Jerusalem said an Israeli soldier took part in the group prayers on the steps of the Dome of the Rock, which marked the start of the Hebrew month, according to Quds News Network. Muslim worshippers were kept from the areas where the groups were and from the route they took, the network reported. WAFA did not give a number of entrants for Monday.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-settlers-cut-power-to-two-west-bank-communities.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"08:53","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers cut power to two West Bank communities, for the fifth and sixth time","g":"settlers","x":"Local officials in the Nablus and Hebron areas said settlers from nearby outposts keep cutting the lines to Palestinian homes.","s":"WAFA / Birin village council","sp":["WAFA","Birin village council"],"p":"al-Mas'udiya, northwest of Nablus; Wadi al-A'war, east of Hebron","b":"Settlers cut the electricity supply to the al-Mas’udiya area on the lands of Burqa, northwest of Nablus, on Monday, the fifth time in a little over two months, the local official responsible for defending the area’s land told WAFA. He said attacks on the area had escalated since an outpost was set up nearby. In Wadi al-A’war, east of Hebron, armed settlers from a newly built outpost cut the power lines feeding about 20 homes for the sixth consecutive time, Birin village council head Farid Burqan told WAFA. He said residents also face demolitions, land bulldozing and movement restrictions aimed at forcing them to leave. WAFA noted that a 79-year-old woman in the area had been moderately injured about three days earlier when a dog set on her by settlers bit her thigh.","n":[["20","homes in Wadi al-A’war whose power lines settlers cut, for the sixth time","Birin village council (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-settlers-raise-shelters-and-flags-on-the-tubas.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"09:23","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers raise shelters and flags on the Tubas plain as troops install a new iron gate","g":"settlers","x":"Residents fear the shelters at Ainun are the start of a new outpost, local sources said.","s":"WAFA (local sources)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Ainun, Tubas plain, and Khallet Abu Salah near Atouf, Tubas governorate","b":"Settlers protected by the Israeli army put up shade shelters and raised Israeli flags at Ainun on the Tubas plain on Monday, local sources told WAFA, and residents fear it is the nucleus of a new outpost. In the afternoon Israeli forces began installing an iron gate at Khallet Abu Salah, between the Tubas plain and Atouf, local sources said, adding that it would further restrict residents’ movement. The area had seen a heavy presence of settlers and soldiers since the morning.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israeli-forces-detain-27-in-pre-dawn-raids-including.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"09:26","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces detain 27 in pre-dawn raids, including two women from one Jenin family","g":"detention","x":"Arrests were reported in Tulkarm, Jenin, Hebron, Bethlehem, Nablus and al-Eizariya, and a man was wounded in Qabatiya.","s":"Quds News Network; WAFA / Palestinian Prisoners' Society","sp":["Quds News Network","WAFA","Palestinian Prisoners' Society"],"p":"Occupied West Bank and East Jerusalem","b":"Israeli forces detained 27 Palestinians, among them former prisoners, in raids across the occupied West Bank and East Jerusalem before dawn on Monday, Quds News Network reported: nine in Tulkarm governorate, six in Jenin, five in Hebron, four in Bethlehem, two in Nablus and one in al-Eizariya. A man was moderately wounded during a house raid in Qabatiya, south of Jenin, the network said. In Fahma, south of Jenin, troops detained four siblings, two of them women, and in Zububa they detained a father and his son, the Palestinian Prisoners’ Society told WAFA. In Bethlehem governorate they detained three men in Jannata and one in Husan, and held young men for field interrogation in al-Arouj, east of Bethlehem, before releasing them, a security source said. WAFA also reported the arrest of a university student in Deir al-Ghusun, north of Tulkarm, a former prisoner in Beit Rima, northwest of Ramallah, and a man in Nablus, and a house raid in Ras al-Far’a, south of Tubas, with no arrests.","n":[["27","Palestinians detained in pre-dawn raids across the West Bank and East Jerusalem","Quds News Network"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israels-high-court-asks-for-a-timetable-to.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"09:51","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israel’s High Court asks for a timetable to return more than 33,000 displaced to northern West Bank camps","g":"humanitarian","x":"The court accepted the state’s view that conditions are not yet ripe, but told it to say by 23 November whether returns can be sped up.","s":"The Times of Israel; Haaretz","sp":["The Times of Israel","Haaretz"],"p":"Jenin, Tulkarm and Nur Shams refugee camps, northern West Bank","b":"Israel’s High Court of Justice ordered the Defense Ministry and the military on Monday to provide a timetable for the return of Palestinians displaced from refugee camps in the northern occupied West Bank, while accepting that security conditions were not yet ripe for them to go back, The Times of Israel reported. Responding to a petition by the Association for Civil Rights in Israel, the court told the state to say by 23 November whether it can speed up the return of more than 33,000 Palestinians displaced from the Jenin, Tulkarm and Nur Shams camps, Haaretz reported. Finance Minister Bezalel Smotrich criticised the ruling, according to The Times of Israel, which said the camps’ residents were displaced by an Israeli military operation in 2025.","n":[["33,000+","Palestinians displaced from the Jenin, Tulkarm and Nur Shams camps","Haaretz (on the High Court petition)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israeli-forces-put-three-iron-gates-around-yabad.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"10:31","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces put three iron gates around Ya’bad and start a military road into Jenin camp","g":"checkpoint","x":"Ya’bad municipality said the gates endanger lives by delaying ambulances; in Jenin, bulldozers began a road on land seized under a new military order.","s":"WAFA / Ya'bad municipality; Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission","sp":["WAFA","Ya'bad municipality","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission"],"p":"Ya'bad, southwest of Jenin; al-Jabriyat, Jenin city","b":"Israeli forces installed three iron gates on Monday at the entrances to Ya’bad, southwest of Jenin, and on the roads to Kufeirit and Kafr Qud, Ya’bad municipality told WAFA. It said another gate on the town’s western side had been closed for more than 20 days. The municipality said the gates delay residents on their way to work, school and clinics, hold up ambulances, and further isolate the town and surrounding villages. Israeli forces had earlier installed more than ten gates at village entrances east of Jenin, WAFA said. In the afternoon Israeli bulldozers began cutting a new road linking the al-Jabriyat military post to Jenin refugee camp, after taking 741 square metres of Palestinian land for ‘military purposes’, WAFA reported. The Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission said the land was seized under military order 33/26, dated 5 October, in an area of Jenin classified as Area A, and that the order allows 24 hours for objections after a site visit with the landowners.","n":[["3","new iron gates around Ya’bad","Ya'bad municipality (via WAFA)"],["741 m²","land seized for a military road from al-Jabriyat to Jenin camp","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israeli-intelligence-soldiers-describe-loosened-rules-for-strikes.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"10:31","r":"gaza","k":"Report","h":"Israeli intelligence soldiers describe loosened rules for strikes in Gaza, Haaretz reports","g":"killed-injured","x":"Four soldiers told the paper that strikes went ahead despite estimates of hundreds of civilian casualties; the military disputes their account.","s":"Haaretz; WAFA; Quds News Network","sp":["Haaretz","WAFA","Quds News Network"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"Four Israeli soldiers who served in military intelligence and drone units told Haaretz that rules for strikes in Gaza were loosened during the war, with attacks approved despite estimates that hundreds of civilians could be harmed, and with orders not to question their missions. The Israeli military disputes their account of how strikes were approved, Haaretz said. According to WAFA’s summary of the report, one soldier said that before a strike on a residential block in Jabalia camp on 31 October 2023, intelligence estimated that 250 to 300 people could be harmed, which sent the decision to the chief of staff; afterwards, he said, it became clear that about 250 Palestinian civilians had been killed. Quds News Network said the report quoted the head of the Israeli group Breaking the Silence calling for those responsible to be held to account.","q":"Even the lowest-ranking target justified civilian casualties","w":"Israeli intelligence soldiers, as quoted in Haaretz's headline","n":[["250-300","people intelligence estimated could be harmed in one October 2023 strike on Jabalia camp, per a soldier quoted by Haaretz","Haaretz (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israel-land-authority-says-e1-settlement-bids-will.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"11:16","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"Israel Land Authority says E1 settlement bids will wait until after the election","g":"settlers","x":"Tenders for 1,234 housing units will not be reviewed before 19 November, after the attorney general ordered a delay.","s":"The Times of Israel / Israel Land Authority","sp":["The Times of Israel","Israel Land Authority"],"p":"E1 area, between Ma'ale Adumim settlement and occupied East Jerusalem","b":"The Israel Land Authority said tenders for 1,234 housing units in the E1 settlement project, east of occupied East Jerusalem, will not be reviewed before 19 November, The Times of Israel reported. The delay follows an order by Israel’s attorney general over concern that the process could be used for campaigning before the 27 October election, the paper said. E1 lies between the Ma’ale Adumim settlement and Jerusalem.","n":[["1,234","E1 housing units whose tender bids will not be reviewed before 19 Nov","Israel Land Authority (via The Times of Israel)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-beit-ula-buries-a-man-who-died-after.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"11:39","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Beit Ula buries a man who died after Israeli forces chased his car","g":"killed-injured","x":"Israel held the 32-year-old’s body from Friday to Sunday, WAFA says.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"Beit Ula, west of Hebron","b":"Mourners in Beit Ula, west of Hebron, buried a 32-year-old man on Monday whose body Israeli forces had held from Friday until Sunday, WAFA reported. The funeral procession set out from the President Mahmoud Abbas Hospital in Halhul to his family’s home. WAFA said he died of injuries sustained when the car he was in overturned while being chased by Israeli forces on the bypass road between the Bethlehem and Hebron governorates."},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-gaza-death-toll-since-october-2023-reaches-74382.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"12:30","r":"gaza","k":"Figures","h":"Gaza death toll since October 2023 reaches 74,382, medical sources say","g":"killed-injured","x":"Hospitals received five bodies and 18 injured in 24 hours; 1,493 people have been killed since the ceasefire, the sources said.","s":"WAFA / Gaza medical sources","sp":["WAFA","Gaza medical sources"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"The number of Palestinians killed in Gaza since 7 October 2023 has risen to 74,382, with 175,416 injured, medical sources told WAFA on Monday. Hospitals received five dead and 18 injured in the previous 24 hours, four of them newly killed and two bodies recovered, the sources said. Since the ceasefire of 11 October 2025, 1,493 people have been killed and 5,299 injured, and 836 bodies have been recovered, they said, adding that some victims remain under rubble or in the streets where rescue crews cannot reach them.","n":[["74,382","Palestinians killed in Gaza since 7 Oct 2023 (175,416 injured)","Gaza medical sources (via WAFA)"],["1,493","killed since the 11 Oct 2025 ceasefire (5,299 injured)","Gaza medical sources (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-tulkarms-governor-tells-ben-gvir-in-haaretz-dont-turn.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"12:59","r":"west-bank","k":"Statement","h":"Tulkarm’s governor tells Ben-Gvir in Haaretz: don’t turn us into fuel for your campaign","g":"diplomacy","x":"Abdallah Kamil wrote that the minister’s remarks near Tulkarm used the 7 October anniversary to spread fear rather than prevent another attack.","s":"Haaretz (opinion by the Governor of Tulkarm)","sp":["Haaretz"],"p":"Tulkarm","b":"Tulkarm Governor Abdallah Kamil accused Israel’s National Security Minister Itamar Ben-Gvir of ‘scaremongering against Palestinians and turning security concerns into a tool for political and electoral gain’, in an opinion piece published by Haaretz on Monday. Kamil was responding to remarks Ben-Gvir made near Tulkarm, which the governor said called for the Palestinian Authority to be dismantled, disarmed and its members detained. On the anniversary of 7 October, he wrote, the message should have been about preventing such a tragedy from happening again, not about producing more fear, incitement and escalation.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israeli-bulldozers-uproot-more-than-200-ancient-olive.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"13:45","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli bulldozers uproot more than 200 ancient olive trees in al-Lubban al-Sharqiya before harvest","g":"olive-harvest","x":"Owners lost this season’s crop; the village’s main entrance was also closed, WAFA’s correspondent said.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"al-Lubban al-Sharqiya, south of Nablus","b":"Israeli bulldozers uprooted more than 200 old, fruit-bearing olive trees in the al-Sur area east of al-Lubban al-Sharqiya, south of Nablus, on Monday before their owners could harvest them, WAFA’s correspondent reported. Israeli forces also closed the village’s main entrance, forcing residents onto detours, and have kept villagers from their trees in Wadi Abwein, southwest of the village, and Wadi al-Sha’ir, to the north, since the start of the season, WAFA said. That evening settlers attacked a man from the village on the Wadi al-Sha’ir road between al-Lubban al-Sharqiya and Salfit and badly damaged his car until residents reached him and took him to safety, local sources told WAFA.","n":[["200+","ancient olive trees uprooted in al-Lubban al-Sharqiya","WAFA"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-commission-counts-219-attacks-in-12-days-of.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"14:35","r":"west-bank","k":"Figures","h":"Commission counts 219 attacks in 12 days of October, 136 of them tied to the olive harvest","g":"olive-harvest","x":"Settlers carried out 177 of the attacks and killed three Palestinians since the start of the month, the Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission said.","s":"WAFA / Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission","sp":["WAFA","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission"],"p":"Occupied West Bank","b":"The Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission recorded 219 attacks by settlers and the Israeli army between 1 and 12 October in a preliminary count based on field reports, 136 of them linked to the olive harvest, it said on Monday. Settlers carried out 177 of the attacks and settlers and soldiers acted together in 42, it said, and settlers have killed three Palestinians since the start of the month. Of the harvest-related incidents, 92 were direct attacks on pickers or denial of access to land (59 by settlers, 19 joint and 14 by the army alone), and 45 involved theft of the crop or damage to trees, the Commission said. It said harvest-season attacks rose from 136 in 2022 to 333 in 2023 and 407 in 2024, and exceeded 441 in 2025.","n":[["219","attacks by settlers and the army, 1-12 Oct (preliminary)","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"],["136","of them linked to the olive harvest","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"],["3","Palestinians killed by settlers since 1 Oct","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-settlers-block-masafer-yatta-schoolchildren-from-getting-home.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"15:08","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Settlers block Masafer Yatta schoolchildren from getting home and attack an olive-picking family","g":"settlers","x":"Pupils who must travel daily to a school in al-Fakheit were forced back to it, a local activist said; nearby, soldiers escorted another family’s children to school.","s":"WAFA (local activist); The Times of Israel","sp":["WAFA","The Times of Israel"],"p":"Khirbet Qawawis and Khirbet al-Halawa, Masafer Yatta, south of Hebron","b":"Settlers attacked members of a family picking olives in Khirbet Qawawis, in Masafer Yatta south of Hebron, on Monday, tried to seize their equipment and stopped them from continuing the harvest, a local activist told WAFA. Another group of settlers blocked the road for schoolchildren trying to get home to Khirbet al-Halawa and stopped vehicles from reaching them, forcing the pupils back to their school in al-Fakheit, the activist said. He said children from the scattered hamlets must travel to al-Fakheit every day because Israel does not allow schools to be built in their communities, and that they are regularly attacked on the way. In Wadi Rahim, in the same South Hebron Hills area, the four children of a Palestinian family whose home settlers had blocked for a week went to school for the first time in that week, escorted by Israeli soldiers, The Times of Israel reported. The army has repeatedly removed settler outposts near the home, but settlers rebuilt them each time, the paper said.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israel-renews-detention-without-charge-for-a-woman.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"15:32","r":"prisoners","k":"Update","h":"Israel renews detention without charge for a woman journalist held in isolation","g":"prisoners","x":"The Prisoners’ Society says 87 Palestinian women are now in Israeli prisons, 21 of them under administrative detention.","s":"Quds News Network / Palestinian Prisoners' Society","sp":["Quds News Network","Palestinian Prisoners' Society"],"p":"Israeli prisons","b":"Israeli authorities renewed for four more months the administrative detention, without charge or trial, of a 33-year-old Palestinian journalist who has been held in isolation for 50 days in a prison in Beersheba, the Palestinian Prisoners’ Society said on Monday, according to Quds News Network. The society said she had been beaten and repeatedly moved between prisons, which it said obstructs lawyers’ visits. The number of women held under administrative detention rose to 21 after a four-month order was issued against a teacher and community activist from al-Bireh, bringing the total number of Palestinian women in Israeli prisons to 87, the society said. In the evening an Israeli court extended until next month the detention of another Palestinian journalist, from Dheisheh camp in Bethlehem, and of her husband, Quds News Network reported. The Prisoners’ Media Office says 38 Palestinian journalists are held in Israeli prisons, 20 of them under administrative detention, the network said.","n":[["87","Palestinian women held in Israeli prisons, 21 of them in administrative detention","Palestinian Prisoners' Society (via Quds News Network)"],["38","Palestinian journalists in Israeli prisons, 20 in administrative detention","Prisoners' Media Office (via Quds News Network)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-vice-president-al-sheikh-says-elections-will-be-brought.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"17:34","r":"west-bank","k":"Statement","h":"Vice President al-Sheikh says elections will be brought forward once conditions allow","g":"diplomacy","x":"He asked the world to press Israel to lift more than 1,000 checkpoints; the EU’s foreign policy chief said the postponed vote should happen as soon as possible.","s":"WAFA; The Times of Israel","sp":["WAFA","The Times of Israel"],"p":"Ramallah","b":"Palestinian Vice President Hussein al-Sheikh said on Monday that the leadership remains committed to holding presidential and legislative elections together and would bring the date forward as soon as conditions allow a fair and transparent vote, WAFA reported. The elections were postponed by presidential decree on 10 October to September 2027. Al-Sheikh called on the international community to press Israel to allow voting in East Jerusalem, the West Bank and Gaza by halting raids and settler attacks, removing ‘more than a thousand checkpoints’ and releasing withheld Palestinian funds, according to WAFA. Earlier in the day al-Sheikh met senior US official Jake McGee in Ramallah, The Times of Israel reported, and Prime Minister Mohammad Mustafa also met a US delegation that included McGee and the US security coordinator, WAFA said. The EU’s foreign policy chief, Kaja Kallas, said the postponed elections should take place as soon as possible, The Times of Israel reported.","q":"Elections are not a mere formality, but a fundamental matter that expresses the will of a people.","w":"Hussein al-Sheikh, Vice President of the State of Palestine","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israeli-forces-demolish-a-memorial-to-the-dead.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"17:53","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli forces demolish a memorial to the dead in Abu Falah as settlers stone cars nearby","g":"demolition","x":"An army bulldozer destroyed the monument in the village centre, local sources said.","s":"WAFA (local sources); al-Mughayyir village council","sp":["WAFA","al-Mughayyir village council"],"p":"Abu Falah and al-Mughayyir, northeast of Ramallah","b":"Israeli forces entered Abu Falah, northeast of Ramallah, with a military bulldozer on Monday evening and demolished a memorial to Palestinians killed in the conflict that stood in the village centre, local sources told WAFA. Troops also entered neighbouring Kafr Malik; no arrests were reported there. Earlier that evening settlers threw stones at Palestinian cars on the Marj Sia’ plain road between al-Mughayyir and Abu Falah, damaging one vehicle, al-Mughayyir village council head Amin Abu Alia told WAFA. Quds News Network said in a report the same day that demolishing memorials, removing flags and arresting people over social media posts form part of a wider Israeli campaign in the West Bank against what it calls incitement."},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israeli-police-detain-two-activists-and-a-settler.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"18:38","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israeli police detain two activists and a settler teen after a clash at a Palestinian olive harvest","g":"settlers","x":"A left-wing group says its volunteers pushed away a settler who tried to attack two Palestinian women; a right-wing site says the activists beat a settler.","s":"The Times of Israel","sp":["The Times of Israel"],"p":"Near Duma, south of Nablus","b":"Israeli police detained two Israeli activists, one of them a reserve brigadier general and former fighter pilot, on suspicion of assaulting a settler shepherd near the Michtam LeDavid farm, close to the Palestinian village of Duma, south of Nablus, The Times of Israel reported. The retired officer was released shortly after questioning, the paper said. The group Looking the Occupation in the Eye said its activists, joined by retired senior officers, had come to help Palestinians pick olives, and that they pushed away a settler who tried to attack two Palestinian women carrying sacks of olives; it said police and soldiers took statements only from settlers. The right-wing site Hakol Hayehudi said three activists kicked, punched and beat a settler with a stick. Police later said a 15-year-old suspected of assault had also been detained, questioned and released, according to The Times of Israel. Police said soldiers had dispersed those involved before officers arrived."},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-israel-orders-three-foreign-activists-deported-after-they.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"18:44","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Israel orders three foreign activists deported after they filmed soldiers in Hebron","g":"detention","x":"Israeli police said one of them heads a French solidarity group and called her a ‘radical activist’.","s":"WAFA / Israeli police statement","sp":["WAFA","Israeli police statement"],"p":"Bab al-Zawiya checkpoint, Hebron","b":"Israeli authorities decided on Monday to deport three foreign activists detained in Hebron on Sunday evening near the Bab al-Zawiya checkpoint, Israeli police said in a statement quoted by WAFA. Police said the three had been filming soldiers and a military post. The Population and Immigration Authority ordered them removed within 24 hours and held in detention until then, the statement said. Police said one of them heads the French group Agir pour la Palestine and is active in the boycott movement, calling her a ‘radical activist’; they did not give the activists’ nationalities.","n":[["3","foreign activists ordered deported","Israeli police (via WAFA)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-un-rights-office-verifies-1076-palestinians-killed-in.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"19:00","r":"gaza","k":"Statement","h":"UN rights office verifies 1,076 Palestinians killed in Gaza between the ceasefire and the end of July","g":"killed-injured","x":"Women and children were 37 percent of the verified deaths; the office says Israeli forces’ conduct near the ‘yellow line’ raises fears it is treated as a kill zone.","s":"UN Human Rights Office - OPT (via WAFA)","sp":["UN Human Rights Office - OPT"],"p":"Gaza Strip","b":"The UN Human Rights Office in the occupied Palestinian territory said it had verified 1,076 conflict-related deaths in Gaza between the ceasefire announcement in October 2025 and the end of July 2026: 268 children, 126 women and 682 men. More than 1,400 Palestinians have been killed since the ceasefire, according to the Palestinian Ministry of Health, the office said. It verified 331 killings near the so-called yellow line marking Israeli military deployment, and said there is no single identifiable line but ever-shifting boundaries, raising serious concerns that the area is being treated as a kill zone. Israeli forces controlled nearly 53 percent of Gaza when the ceasefire was announced and aim to reach 70 percent, the office said. The office said the pattern raises serious concerns that Israel is directing attacks against civilians, which would be a war crime.","q":"Violence of this magnitude is outrageous at any time. To record it during a supposed ceasefire is simply unfathomable.","w":"Ajith Sunghay, Head of UN Human Rights - OPT","n":[["1,076","conflict-related deaths in Gaza verified by the UN, Oct 2025 to end of July 2026 (268 children, 126 women, 682 men)","UN Human Rights Office - OPT"],["331","of them killed near the ‘yellow line’","UN Human Rights Office - OPT (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-jerusalem-man-forced-to-demolish-his-own-home.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"19:11","r":"jerusalem","k":"Update","h":"Jerusalem man forced to demolish his own home; two more homes in Silwan face demolition","g":"demolition","x":"The Beit Hanina home belonged to a man married a year ago and expecting his first child, the Jerusalem Governorate said.","s":"WAFA / Jerusalem Governorate","sp":["WAFA","Jerusalem Governorate"],"p":"Beit Hanina and Silwan, occupied East Jerusalem","b":"Israel’s Jerusalem municipality forced a Palestinian to demolish his own 60-square-metre home in the al-Ashqariya neighbourhood of Beit Hanina on Monday, the Jerusalem Governorate said. The house was built in 2017; its owner married about a year ago and is expecting his first child, the governorate said. Israeli authorities also served demolition orders on the homes of a father and son in the Wadi Qaddum neighbourhood of Silwan, south of Al-Aqsa, the governorate said. The son had begun emptying both homes to demolish them himself; his 100-square-metre house, built in 2002, is home to his wife and four children, and his parents’ house measures 60 square metres. WAFA said Israel rarely grants Palestinians building permits in Jerusalem, and that those who refuse to demolish their homes themselves face demolition by municipal bulldozers and heavy costs.","n":[["60 m²","home in Beit Hanina its owner was forced to demolish","Jerusalem Governorate (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-katz-calls-israels-presence-in-emptied-west-bank.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"19:11","r":"west-bank","k":"Statement","h":"Katz calls Israel’s presence in emptied West Bank camps ‘permanent’ and tells court not to intervene","g":"humanitarian","x":"Israel’s defense minister said he would seek legislation if needed, hours after the High Court asked for a return timetable.","s":"The Times of Israel","sp":["The Times of Israel"],"p":"Jenin, Tulkarm and Nur Shams refugee camps, northern West Bank","b":"Israel’s Defense Minister Israel Katz described the army’s presence in the depopulated refugee camps of the northern occupied West Bank as ‘permanent’ and urged the High Court not to intervene in what he called an operational decision, The Times of Israel reported on Monday evening. Katz said he would not compromise on the issue ‘even if it requires special legislative proceedings in the Knesset’, and claimed that ‘terrorism’ in the West Bank had dropped by 80 percent, the paper said. Earlier in the day the court had asked the state for a timetable for the return of tens of thousands of displaced camp residents.","q":"It is not the High Court’s business to intervene in this sort of security decisions.","w":"Israel Katz, Israeli Defense Minister","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-commission-records-14-attacks-on-farmers-in-four.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"19:18","r":"west-bank","k":"Figures","h":"Commission records 14 attacks on farmers in four governorates in 12 hours","g":"olive-harvest","x":"Eight were in Ramallah and al-Bireh; in Yasuf, settlers stole olives and a harvesting machine.","s":"WAFA / Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission","sp":["WAFA","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission"],"p":"Occupied West Bank","b":"The Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission recorded 14 attacks on farmers, their land and crops between 6 a.m. and 6 p.m. on Monday in the Ramallah and al-Bireh, Salfit, Nablus and Hebron governorates: ten by settlers, three by the army and one carried out jointly, it said. Eight were in Ramallah and al-Bireh, including in Turmus Ayya, Burqa, Kafr Malik and Deir Dibwan, where a farmer’s car was smashed, the Commission said. In Yasuf, east of Salfit, settlers drove farmers off their land and stole olives and a harvesting machine, village council head Jum’a Abdul Fattah told WAFA; settlers also pitched a tent near the Iskaka spring. In Mukhmas, north of Jerusalem, two settlers chased a shepherd and tried to steal his sheep before raiding olive groves and taking the fruit, the Jerusalem Governorate said.","n":[["14","attacks on farmers, land and crops, 06:00-18:00 on 12 Oct","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via WAFA)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-unesco-board-adopts-two-resolutions-on-palestine-unanimously.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"19:33","r":"world","k":"Statement","h":"UNESCO board adopts two resolutions on Palestine unanimously","g":"diplomacy","x":"They cover cultural heritage in Jerusalem, Hebron and Bethlehem and educational institutions in the occupied territories.","s":"WAFA","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"UNESCO headquarters, Paris","b":"UNESCO’s Executive Board, at its 225th session, unanimously adopted two resolutions on Palestine, one on the occupied Palestinian territory and one on educational and cultural institutions in the occupied Arab territories, WAFA reported. They cover the protection of heritage sites in Jerusalem, Hebron and Bethlehem and of schools and cultural institutions. Palestine’s ambassador to UNESCO, Adel Atieh, said attacks on schools, universities, heritage sites and journalists strike at the core of the organisation’s mandate. Palestinian National Council President Rouhi Fattouh welcomed the vote and called for the resolutions to be turned into binding measures, WAFA said."},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-ibrahimi-mosque-director-and-head-of-custodians-held.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"19:47","r":"west-bank","k":"Update","h":"Ibrahimi Mosque director and head of custodians held after refusing to hand over keys, QNN says","g":"detention","x":"The two were moved to Ofer prison and face a hearing this week; the Palestinian Awqaf ministry has not commented, the network said.","s":"Quds News Network","sp":["Quds News Network"],"p":"Ibrahimi Mosque, Old City of Hebron","b":"Israeli forces last Thursday detained the director of the Ibrahimi Mosque in Hebron and the head of its custodians after they refused to hand over the keys to historic shrines inside the building, Quds News Network reported on Monday, citing its own information and the director’s father, an imam at the mosque. The two were detained during an inspection tour of the mosque that Israeli authorities had requested, taken to the Etzion interrogation centre and moved on Sunday to Ofer prison, with a hearing set for next Thursday, the network said. It said the mosque’s staff have been barred for about four months from several shrines and rooms in the section under Israeli control, and that the call to prayer has not been made from its historic room for 113 days. The Palestinian Ministry of Awqaf and Religious Affairs had not issued a statement on the arrests, the network said.","n":[["113","days the call to prayer has not been made from the mosque’s historic call room","Quds News Network (mosque administration sources)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-gaza-civil-defence-warns-of-new-displacement-and.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"21:23","r":"gaza","k":"Statement","h":"Gaza civil defence warns of new displacement and cracked homes near the ‘yellow line’","g":"humanitarian","x":"Blasts and gunfire have damaged tents and forced dozens of families to move again in eastern Gaza City and Khan Younis, it said.","s":"Quds News Network / Gaza Civil Defence","sp":["Quds News Network","Gaza Civil Defence"],"p":"Shuja'iya, al-Tuffah and al-Zeitoun; Bani Suheila and al-Satr al-Sharqi","b":"Gaza’s civil defence said on Monday that heavy explosions and continuous gunfire had displaced dozens of families again and damaged displaced people’s tents and homes in Shuja’iya, al-Tuffah and al-Zeitoun in eastern Gaza City, around the Bani Suheila roundabout, east of the Hamad City towers and in al-Satr al-Sharqi in Khan Younis, Quds News Network reported. The service warned that homes already damaged in earlier strikes could collapse on families who have returned to them, and said alternative shelter areas lack basic necessities. It called on the guarantors of the ceasefire to press Israel to stop the attacks.","ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-besieged-for-a-month-47-families-in-al-ras.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"21:27","r":"west-bank","k":"Story","h":"Besieged for a month, 47 families in al-Ras al-Ahmar face being driven out","g":"settlers","x":"Four families have already left after armed settlers threatened them, and the last road in was cut by a trench, Quds News Network reports.","s":"Quds News Network (report)","sp":["Quds News Network"],"p":"Khirbet al-Ras al-Ahmar, east of Tammun, Tubas governorate","b":"In Khirbet al-Ras al-Ahmar, a herding community east of Tammun in the northern Jordan Valley, 47 Palestinian families have lived under a tight siege for about a month, Quds News Network reported. Four families have already left after armed settlers raided their homes and threatened to kill them, the network said. Residents go out at dawn for supplies and fetch water at night to avoid being chased, and a trench was dug in mid-September across the last remaining way in, the report said. The head of the local village council said in September that Israeli forces had demolished six wells in a month and confiscated pumps, cutting the water lines each time residents relaid them. On 10 October settlers gave the 47 families until the following evening to leave, the network said. It cited the Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission as recording 14,742 settler attacks on 376 Palestinian communities in the three years since 7 October 2023, in which 67 Palestinians were killed.","n":[["47","families in al-Ras al-Ahmar facing displacement","Quds News Network"],["14,742","settler attacks on 376 communities since 7 Oct 2023, killing 67 Palestinians","Colonization and Wall Resistance Commission (via Quds News Network)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-three-principals-and-59-teachers-kept-from-their.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"21:47","r":"west-bank","k":"Statement","h":"Three principals and 59 teachers kept from their schools south of Nablus, education officials say","g":"checkpoint","x":"The education ministry warns that dozens of West Bank schools face demolition or stop-work orders.","s":"Quds News Network / Palestinian Ministry of Education","sp":["Quds News Network","Palestinian Ministry of Education"],"p":"South of Nablus and Masafer Yatta; Gaza Strip","b":"Israeli measures prevented three school principals and 59 teachers from reaching their schools south of Nablus on Monday, Samer al-Jamal, the director of education for south Nablus, said, according to Quds News Network. Ahmad Sawafta, an adviser to the education minister, said dozens of schools face demolition or stop-work orders, and that an Israeli military tower on the road to the Jinba school in Masafer Yatta and a settler kiosk at its gate put pupils at risk, the network reported. Education Minister Amjad Barham has said that more than 90 percent of school buildings and more than 85 percent of university buildings in Gaza were destroyed or damaged, and official figures put the number of students and education staff killed in Gaza and the West Bank at 21,806, the network said.","n":[["59","teachers kept from their schools south of Nablus (plus 3 principals)","Education directorate of south Nablus (via Quds News Network)"]]},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-in-umm-safa-villagers-cannot-reach-the-cemetery.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"22:16","r":"west-bank","k":"Story","h":"In Umm Safa, villagers cannot reach the cemetery to bury their dead","g":"settlers","x":"Settlers have cut the village off from its only cemetery since August, and six outposts now ring it, its council head told Mondoweiss.","s":"Mondoweiss (feature)","sp":["Mondoweiss"],"p":"Umm Safa, north of Ramallah","b":"The roughly 750 people of Umm Safa, north of Ramallah, have been unable to reach their village’s only cemetery since August 2026, when settlers seized the last farmland around it and set up a new outpost there, Mondoweiss reported in a feature by Palestinian journalist Shatha Hammad. ‘If someone in the village dies, there is nowhere to bury them,’ village council head Marwan Sabbah told the outlet. He said six outposts have been set up on the village’s land since October 2023, one of them about 50 metres from homes, and that the village now holds about 100 of its roughly 4,800 dunams. The army has sealed the village’s main entrance with a gate for three years, turning a 20-minute trip to Ramallah into more than an hour, the report said. A 70-year-old mother who lives about 50 metres from an outpost told Mondoweiss that settlers took four of her sheep about a month ago and that soldiers stopped villagers from getting them back. ‘It’s impossible for us to leave Umm Safa,’ she said.","q":"We will not abandon the cemetery where our grandparents, and their grandparents, are buried.","w":"Marwan Sabbah, Head of Umm Safa village council, to Mondoweiss","n":[["6","outposts set up on Umm Safa’s land since October 2023","Umm Safa village council (via Mondoweiss)"],["100 of 4,800","dunams of Umm Safa land still in villagers’ hands","Umm Safa village council (via Mondoweiss)"]],"ph":1},{"u":"news/2026-10-12-wafas-daily-round-up-three-killed-in-gaza-a.html","d":"2026-10-12","t":"23:54","r":"west-bank","k":"Report","h":"WAFA’s daily round-up: three killed in Gaza, a boy shot and detained in Qalqilya","g":"raid","x":"The agency’s closing tally lists arrests, new gates, demolitions and settler attacks across the West Bank.","s":"WAFA (daily round-up)","sp":["WAFA"],"p":"West Bank and Gaza","b":"WAFA’s end-of-day round-up said three Palestinians were killed in Gaza on Monday: two in the drone strike in Rafah’s al-Mawasi area and a man who died of wounds from a strike in the north the day before. In the evening Israeli forces entered Qalqilya from its southern entrance, firing stun grenades and bullets, and shot and wounded a boy before detaining him, local sources told WAFA. Troops also detained a man at his farm in Kafr Malik and a man in Silwad, briefly held a Silwad municipality ambulance volunteer, and fired tear gas in Deir Dibwan, setting off a fire near the town’s main mosque. The round-up said three drivers detained inside the Karem Abu Salem crossing in southeastern Gaza were held for some time and then released. It also listed the gates at Ya’bad and the Tubas plain, the al-Lubban al-Sharqiya uprooting and the Jerusalem demolition orders.","n":[["3","Palestinians killed in Gaza on 12 Oct, per the round-up","WAFA"]],"ph":1}];
